40827771916480570222239393837349515,0.005,0,15.42871499999999999999999999999999999991,35634224,0\\r\\n7a3d2192db1d8c3333c66b6dd67ddb96ab0b7e90ff39c10b4087a9e9d936404b,post28,post,cash_operating_expenses,2.939940827771916480570222239393837349515,[],-0.8978295466666666666666666666666666666665,2.042111281105249813903555572727170682848,0.005,0,15.42871499999999999999999999999999999991,35634224,0\\r\\n7a3d2192db1d8c3333c66b6dd67ddb96ab0b7e90ff39c10b4087a9e9d936404b,post28,post,current_cash_tax,2.042111281105249813903555572727170682848,[],-0.4959668107303275642214719403358210314450,1.546144470374922249682083632391349651403,0.005,0,15.42871499999999999999999999999999999991,35634224,0\\r\\n7a3d2192db1d8c3333c66b6dd67ddb96ab0b7e90ff39c10b4087a9e9d936404b,post28,post,maintenance_and_expansion_capex,1.546144470374922249682083632391349651403,[],-0.3232186368000000000000000000000000000000,1.222925833574922249682083632391349651403,0.005,0,15.42871499999999999999999999999999999991,35634224,0\\r\\n7a3d2192db1d8c3333c66b6dd67ddb96ab0b7e90ff39c10b4087a9e9d936404b,post28,post,principal_repayment_assumed,1.222925833574922249682083632391349651403,[],-0.3488979603810169141670995988355282241677,0.8740278731939053355149840335558214272353,0.005,0,15.42871499999999999999999999999999999991,35634224,0\\r\\n7a3d2192db1d8c3333c66b6dd67ddb96ab0b7e90ff39c10b4087a9e9d936404b,post29,post,front_customer_collections,0.8740278731939053355149840335558214272353,[],2.904558700746194532990118432693434037686,3.778586573940099868505102466249255464921,0.005,0,15.07981703961898308583290040116447177574,35634224,0\\r\\n7a3d2192db1d8c3333c66b6dd67ddb96ab0b7e90ff39c10b4087a9e9d936404b,post29,post,front_supplier_payments,3.778586573940099868505102466249255464921,\\\"[{\\\"\\\"fee\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"0.028530528\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"gross\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"0.9510176\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"net\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"0.922487072\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"price\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"26\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"shares\\\"\\\": 365776, \\\"\\\"type\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"optional_uncommitted_whole_share_equity\\\"\\\"}]\\\",-7.967191699771778233535150800762518425160,-3.266118053831678365030048334513262960239,0.005,3.271118053831678365030048334513262960239,15.07981703961898308583290040116447177574,36000000,0\\r\\n\"","\"# Retaggio Industries Limited: SME research\\n\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**Hold: unresolved financial reliability, funding and tradability. BQS NS; GQS NS; IAS NS.** This is not a recommendation to buy at a low reported P/E. Retaggio's FY26 revenue and PAT expanded sharply, but cash realization, capital reconciliation and several internal contradictions prevent reliance on headline earnings. A formally unmodified audit is not the same as a clean investability assessment.\\n\\nRetaggio supplies jewellery, mainly to business customers. This is an unresolved speculative growth / capital-allocation situation, not an evidence-backed compounder.\\n\\nThe central economic question is whether the ₹64.89 crore March receivable balance is collectible, sufficiently quickly, without repeated outside funding. The second is whether the ₹10 crore October property agreement, plus charges, is supported by available cash and productive incremental returns. The third is how much profit and cash ultimately belongs to each listed share after warrants and the new subsidiary. None is answered by the nominal growth rate alone.\\n\\nEvidence of deliberate fraud, diversion or a severe governance violation has **not** been established. Missing evidence therefore creates holds and NS, not fabricated low scores or an automatic governance-Avoid label. The model is a conditional stress laboratory, not an asserted fair value. No ordinary return is shown for any cash-breached path.\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n- Exact issuer: **Retaggio Industries Limited**, ISIN **INE0KWJ01014**, BSE SME **544391**, CIN L36990MH2022PLC374614. The fourth ISIN character is zero. Listed 7 April 2025.\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, India time**. Market observation cutoff: **19 October 2026**. Retrieval occurred 23 October, with later facts excluded.\\n- Reference ₹52 is a secondary **14 October last-traded price**, not a verified 19 October closing transaction or an executable quote. Do not infer a trade from the midpoint of the exchange security-master band.\\n- Diagnostic market capitalization: ₹106.538432 crore using the stale ₹52 reference and 20,488,160 shares outstanding on 19 October; it is not an executable current valuation.\\n- Scope: standalone through March 2026. June 2026 standalone results are separately retained; the limited-review filing also includes Lodha Heritage in consolidated scope. No artificial five-year consolidated series is created.\\n- FY22 is a dormant incorporation stub; FY23 includes a November business takeover. Only FY24–26 provide three complete fiscal observations, themselves affected by reporting-vintage and economic-comparability problems.\\n- Horizon and hurdles: five years; default 15% nominal annual equity hurdle before investor taxes. The terminal WACC is a separately declared 14% analytical assumption, not a measured cost of capital and not the same as the equity hurdle.\\n- Accounting basis: FY25 uses Indian GAAP Accounting Standards; FY26 describes Ind AS. A reliable transition/reconciliation was not established.\\n- Reference implementation test: ₹5 lakh, not the user's portfolio allocation. Official 19 October interoperability file shows a 3,000-share lot, but stale issued capital and encoded restrictions cannot replace a current BSE tradability check.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Required evidence to clear |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 Governance | Hold | Reconcile conflicting audit, capital, related-party and control disclosures; distinguish errors from misconduct. |\\n| G2 Financial reliability | Hold | Corrected equity total and cash/issuance bridge; reliable accounting-basis transition; reconciled audit trail, IFC and CARO findings. IAS NS and no quality ranking. |\\n| G3 Funding survival | Hold | Current bank cash, facilities and maturities; actual receivable collections; property payment schedule and committed funding. Scenario survival is not proof. |\\n| G4 Tradability | Hold | Complete official 20/60-session turnover, zero/no-trade days, spread, restrictions and executable lot/position capacity. |\\n\\nDocument confidence varies. Original financial statements and signed corporate-event details are primary facts, but several facts within them conflict. Current funding, true organic growth and forward earnings economics are Low confidence. Reported financial arithmetic is Medium-confidence transcription, not certification of underlying balances.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\n| Aggregate | Known contribution | Coverage | Raw bound | Effective bound | Final |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | 1.20 | 40% | 1.80–7.20 | 1.80–7.20 | NS |\\n| GQS | 1.00 | 30% | 1.70–8.00 | 1.70–5.00 | NS |\\n| IAS | 0.00 | 0% | 1.00–10.00 | 1.00–10.00 | NS |\\n\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Primary reason |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| GOV | NS | Low | Stewardship is not reliably scoreable before reconciliation; no substantiated severe-abuse override is asserted. |\\n| MOAT | 3 | Medium | Customer bargaining and replication risks are material; no verified durable pricing, brand or cost advantage. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | No defensible three- or five-year underlying comparable growth basis; nominal growth does not justify a starting score 9. |\\n| DUR | 4 | Medium | Some accessible runway exists, but unsupported customer conversion/expansion keeps this below the adequate band; returns are assessed separately. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | Additional-capital denominators and normalized earnings are unreliable; diagnostic ROIC is not a scoreable economic return. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Medium | Repeated weak realization in FY25–26 is not adequately explained as a temporary build; the GQS cap 5 applies, without replacing NS. |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | Current funding resilience is unscoreable; hypothetical surviving branches do not establish actual solvency. |\\n| EXEC | NS | Low | No reliable Medium-confidence delivery record exists for the enlarged operating scale. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | Normalized operating margins, tax and fully diluted per-share earnings are not established; headline PAT growth is not scoreable. |\\n| R | NS | Low | No supported return model or executable entry quote; illustrative returns cannot determine R. |\\n| D | NS | Low | Loss severity and actual funding resilience cannot be scored from conditional scenarios alone. |\\n| P | NS | Low | No supported price-implied-expectations score before the operating and funding base is established. |\\n| L | NS | Low | Liquidity and the ₹5 lakh reference exit test remain unscoreable; missing sessions are not zero trades. |\\n\\n\\nAll 13 canonical factors appear once. BQS uses GOV 20%, MOAT 20%, REV 12%, DUR 10%, CAP 12%, CASH 10%, BAL 8%, EXEC 8%. GQS uses REV 40%, DUR 20%, EARN 15%, CAP 15%, CASH 10%. IAS uses **unrounded final BQS** 25%, R 35%, D 20%, P 10%, L 10%. Decimal arithmetic and conventional half-up one-decimal presentation are used. No unknown weight is redistributed and no midpoint is invented.\\n\\nBQS known contribution is 0.20×3 + 0.10×4 + 0.10×2 = 1.20, missing weight 0.60, bound [1.80, 7.20]. GQS contribution is 0.20×4 + 0.10×2 = 1.00, missing weight 0.70, raw bound [1.70, 8.00]. The persistent weak-cash-realization override caps the computable upper outcome at 5, but **GQS stays NS**, not 5.0. FY23 cash contamination is not needed to trigger that override: FY25 conversion was weak and FY26 strongly negative even before questioning the positive unexplained inflow.\\n\\nIAS has 0% point-score coverage because Q is NS and all four investment factors are NS. Its prescribed missing-data bound is [1,10]; propagating BQS's narrower mathematical bound gives an additional conditional [1.20,9.30], not a score or confidence interval. A one-point MOAT change moves BQS's known contribution by 0.20; a one-point DUR change moves BQS by 0.10 and GQS by 0.20. These cannot repair missing evidence. No GOV≤3/G1-fail or G3-fail cap is asserted without adequate evidence. No ranking is permitted under the G2 hold.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nRetaggio sells gold and diamond jewellery, precious stones, other fancy jewellery and bullion, primarily B2B. The promoter's Vaibhav Gems business began in 2012 and was transferred under the November 2022 business-transfer arrangement. That history is useful operating context, not a listed-company five-year audited record. Shares issued as takeover consideration are capital employed, not free cash raised. The IPO issued 6,198,000 shares at ₹25, raising ₹15.495 crore gross; the prospectus allocated ₹11.50 crore to working capital and ₹1.90 crore to debt repayment, with ₹13.40 crore net objects. Those proceeds precede the March 2026 model anchor and are never added again.\\n\\nThe IPO business description emphasizes design, craftsmanship, customer orders and a factory at Options Primo, Mumbai. However, FY26 Note 24 classifies ₹83.8245 crore as traded goods and just ₹0.16 crore as labour receipts. Manufacturing narrative, purchase accounting and actual value added need reconciliation. Revenue is a poor standalone demand indicator when precious-metal prices and mix can move gross invoice value.\\n\\nThe prospectus reports FY24 capacity of 975,000 pieces, output 328,250, utilization 33.67%; September-half output 166,150 against 487,500 capacity, 34.08%. It lists 15 employees in March 2025; FY26 reports five permanent employees. No equivalent FY26 physical output, grams sold, outsourcing split, customer reorder cohorts or plant-level contribution margin was established. Fewer employees need not mean contraction in a trading/outsourcing model, but they do not verify scalable manufacturing either.\\n\\nLodha Heritage Private Limited was incorporated after March 2026. The April announcement summary specifies 76% subscription to ₹10 lakh initial capital and a proposed transition of manufacturing/retail operations. Original ownership confirmation is tracked separately; the Q1 original review confirms subsidiary scope, not wholly owned status. The diagnostic base assigns half the operations to that 76%-owned vehicle, hence a 12% economic minority interest, with 0%/24% sensitivities. This is an assumption, not an observed segment allocation.\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\nRelationships, design familiarity and the predecessor's operating history can help win repeat orders. They are not demonstrated switching costs, consumer brand pricing power, network effects or proprietary technology. The prospectus says there are no material customer contracts. FY26's credit-risk note reports five customers representing all revenue, although surrounding note inconsistencies require confirmation of that concentration.\\n\\nSmall workshop capacity and unused installed capacity mean supply is not obviously the constraint. Collection discipline, customer access, metal funding and mix are more important. Industry demand can grow while a small supplier earns little economic profit or finances its customers. This distinction keeps DUR separate from CAP. No peer multiple is used to imply a moat, and no national jewellery TAM is assigned to this issuer as accessible revenue. Prospectus risk 12 says jewellery designs are produced by job workers and are not registered under the Designs Act, leaving copying risk; this is stronger evidence against a proprietary-design moat than a generic industry assertion.\\n\\nCompetitive-position score 3 reflects contestable supply and weak contractual protection, not the audit problems already assigned to G2/GOV. Contrary evidence includes operating longevity and repeat-relationship potential; evidence of sustained excess returns or premium realizations is still missing.\\n\\n### Named economic peers, not a peer-average valuation\\n\\nThe peer window is deliberately fixed at FY25, a common completed period available before the cutoff. This is a structural business-model comparison, not a current trading screen or full forensic clearance of the peers. Stronger/weaker labels refer only to the specified channel, scale or reported margin dimension.\\n\\n| Peer / FY25 source | Primary operating evidence | Relative economic comparison | Comparability limits |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| [Sky Gold and Diamonds Limited](https://skygold.co.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annual-Report_Sky-Gold-and-Diamonds-Limited-2024-25.pdf) | B2B supply to jewellery retailers/wholesalers; disclosed capacity of 1,050 kg/month. FY25 consolidated revenue was ₹354,801.96 lakh and PAT ₹13,265.53 lakh; acquisitions of Sparkling Chains and Starmangalsutra expanded the product mix. | Stronger disclosed industrial scale and product/customer access than Retaggio; its manufacturing-supplier model is economically closer than a pure branded retailer. | Consolidated acquisition scope differs from Retaggio standalone. Reported PAT margin 3.74% illustrates that scale and metal-pass-through revenue do not guarantee high margins. No valuation multiple is transferred. PDF 3, 5, 7, 30; printed 58–59 financial highlights and printed 180 consolidated P&L. |\\n| [RBZ Jewellers Limited](https://rbzjewellers.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annaul-Report-2024-2025.pdf) | Integrated antique-gold manufacturing, wholesale/job work and Harit Zaveri retail. FY25 mix: wholesale 37% of revenue / 20% of volume; job work 2% / 50%; retail 61% / 30%. Revenue was ₹53,014.85 lakh and PAT before OCI ₹3,879.85 lakh. | Stronger disclosed direct-customer channel and mix visibility; the job-work volume/revenue disparity is a concrete warning against treating gross revenue as physical demand. | Retail integration and antique-gold specialization make its 7.32% PAT margin non-transferable to Retaggio. Not a like-for-like market-multiple or consolidated-margin comparison. PDF 9–10 operating model; PDF 32 financial results; revised report 10 September 2025. |\\n| [Moksh Ornaments Limited](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/AR_28914_MOKSH_2024_2025_A_2812467_08092025171608.pdf) | FY25 Note 22 separates domestic goods of ₹47,641.35 lakh, export sales ₹10,324.50 lakh and services ₹24.35 lakh. Main P&L revenue of ₹57,990.20 lakh and PAT ₹842.76 lakh imply a 1.45% PAT margin. | A weaker-margin comparator than RBZ and Sky on the same reported FY25 measure, despite substantial gross sales. It tests the assumption that jewellery-supply scale necessarily produces pricing power. | Different product/export mix, financing and accounting basis. The board headline of ₹58,251.51 lakh differs from main P&L/Note 22, so those identified statement values are used, not a blended number. No full peer-quality clearance is claimed. PDF 56 main P&L; PDF 66 Note 22; PDF 16 board headline retained as a discrepancy. |\\n\\n\\nRetaggio has not demonstrated Sky’s disclosed industrial scale or RBZ’s direct-customer channel. Moksh supplies a weaker-margin counterexample, not evidence that Retaggio deserves a premium: Retaggio’s own reported margin cannot establish superiority while G2 remains unresolved. Gold pass-through, product/export mix, job-work revenue recognition, acquisitions, retail integration and financing differ. No peer P/E, average margin or branded-retail multiple is transferred into Retaggio’s valuation.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\nFY23 restated revenue was ₹23.0659 crore; FY24 ₹23.2783 crore; FY25 ₹23.4921 crore; FY26 ₹83.9845 crore. FY26 nominal growth was about 257.5%. A three-interval FY23–26 CAGR can be calculated, but the first period contains a partial-year transfer and cannot establish organic demand. The FY24–26 two-interval CAGR is also only nominal. Five-year revenue CAGR is ND, not zero.\\n\\n| Nominal revenue interval | Start / end revenue (₹ crore) | Annual ₹ addition or total interval addition | Exact interval count | Growth / CAGR | Interpretation |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| FY22–FY23 | 0 / 23.0659 | 23.0659 | 1 | NM | NM growth: dormant stub and partial business takeover |\\n| FY23–FY24 | 23.0659 / 23.2783 | 0.2124 | 1 | 0.92% | Nominal only; partial-year acquisition base |\\n| FY24–FY25 | 23.2783 / 23.4921 | 0.2138 | 1 | 0.92% | Nominal reported revenue, not price/volume growth |\\n| FY25–FY26 | 23.4921 / 83.9845 | 60.4924 | 1 | 257.50% | Nominal reported revenue, major pass-through/mix uncertainty |\\n| FY24–FY26 | 23.2783 / 83.9845 | 60.7062 | 2 | 89.94% | Two-year CAGR, not the preferred comparable organic window |\\n| FY23–FY26 | 23.0659 / 83.9845 | 60.9186 | 3 | 53.84% | Three-year arithmetic CAGR only; partial takeover base |\\n| Three-year / five-year organic | Not established | Not established | 3 / 5 | NS / NS | No comparable volume/constant-price, acquisition-adjusted series; no invented endpoints. |\\n\\n\\nThe product mix changed materially: FY24 prospectus revenue included gold 24KT ₹3.0329 crore, 18KT ₹6.1738 crore, 22KT ₹5.2710 crore and diamond jewellery ₹8.8006 crore. H1 FY25 reported 18KT ₹3.3446 crore, diamonds ₹5.6141 crore and job work ₹0.0702 crore. No price/volume bridge reconciles these with FY26's traded-goods surge. Geographic percentages also conflict between prospectus risk and business sections; the report does not select the more attractive version.\\n\\nQ1 FY27 original standalone results: revenue ₹13.1518 crore, EBITDA ₹1.6504 crore, interest ₹0.1806 crore, depreciation ₹0.0216 crore, PBT ₹1.4482 crore, tax ₹0.2172 crore and PAT ₹1.2310 crore. Q1 FY26 comparator revenue was ₹2.6720 crore and PAT a loss of ₹0.1386 crore. Q4 FY26 revenue was ₹51.4367 crore: a very large portion of annual sales arrived late in the year, consistent with high year-end receivables. Q1 is not annualized as a supported forecast and does not earn a trajectory-score upgrade.\\n\\nREV remains NS under the framework's explicit gross-price/pass-through and acquisition-comparability rule. A naïve ≥30% growth starting score of 9 would be inappropriate. The comparison uses declared periods rather than shopping for the highest CAGR.\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nThe accessible runway depends on customers paying, incremental orders carrying adequate contribution and the new property producing more than its cost of capital. Capacity headroom alone is insufficient. IPO proceeds and subsequent warrants have expanded financial capital much faster than the audited physical asset base.\\n\\nThe 16 October property agreement specifies ₹10 crore consideration plus stamp duty, registration and other actual charges for 3,261.65 square feet from M S Mody Builders and Developers. Management says it is not a related-party transaction. Payment instalments, amount already paid, unit economics, incremental production and rental alternatives were not disclosed in the retrieved notice. The model tests payment before entry versus immediately after the known 20 October conversion, with assumed ₹0.7 crore charges and ₹0/₹1 crore charge sensitivities. It does not invent a paid balance or treat the property as simultaneously productive operating capital and a separate terminal windfall.\\n\\nExisting ₹3.2 crore noncurrent property receives no incremental recovery value in the core model because title, liquidity, rental income and independent valuation are unverified. A future verified recovery can be a separately funded scenario, not a concealed valuation plug. Capitalizing business-transfer consideration and recognizing diluted shares is necessary even when no current cash was paid.\\n\\nCAP is NS. Diagnostic financing-side invested capital equals arithmetic equity plus debt less cash, including warrant money and noncash takeover capital. It is not clean operating invested capital: advances/property classifications, current liabilities, original-vs-restated reserves and the FY26 equity error matter. NOPAT at an assumed 25% tax is a transparent proxy, not normalized reported profit. Three-/five-year economic incremental ROIC is NS; the one-year numerator/denominator bridge is retained for inspection rather than elevated into a score.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\nSavinay Lodha is managing director and became CFO after the December 2025 CFO resignation, concentrating management and financial responsibility. Original reports disclose the promoter business transfer and noncash director transactions; the historical CARO states Section 192 compliance. These facts require scrutiny but do not by themselves establish misconduct.\\n\\nThe April 2026 postal notice proposed increasing the managing director’s salary from ₹24 lakh to ₹60 lakh annually, effective 1 April 2026, with perquisites limited to an equal amount. This is a proposed authorization in the retrieved notice, not proof of actual cash paid or an independently verified vote outcome. The salary alone is about 6.7% of reported FY26 PAT, versus roughly 239% of FY25 tax-reclassified CFO; potential perquisites are additional. The FY26 board ratio reports 8:1 remuneration to median employee pay while the RPT table says nil managerial remuneration, so a clean five-year remuneration comparison cannot be asserted.\\n\\nAt IPO, the issuer disclosed four company tax matters totalling ₹85.68 lakh and 12 promoter/director matters totalling ₹359.17 lakh. Director amounts are not automatically company liabilities. The prospectus also describes pending registration of the promoter-owned office transfer and factory-license/registration formalities. These are title/compliance questions to close, not established misconduct.\\n\\nMaterial issues requiring written reconciliation:\\n\\n1. FY26 board audit-trail assurance contradicts the main audit's statement that audit-trail software was not used/operational throughout. Main audit says IFC reporting is exempt; a separate annexure nevertheless gives a positive IFC opinion. The latter contradiction also appears in FY25.\\n2. FY26 CARO says no IPO and no preferential/private placement despite the April 2025 IPO and January/March 2026 warrant transactions. A no-working-capital-limit-above-₹5-crore clause is difficult to reconcile with the earlier ₹7 crore cash-credit limit, but outstanding bank debt alone is not proof of a current sanctioned limit.\\n3. Authorized-share and warrant-note figures conflict with board resolutions and signed allotment notices. Current dilution uses the latter, while legal rescue headroom remains conditional on confirmation.\\n4. RPT aggregate tables showing nil loans/remuneration conflict with ₹5.48 lakh related loans and other remuneration disclosures. FY25 related advances and a promoter receivable need a complete cash/terms/repayment ledger, not an assumed zero exposure.\\n5. The FY26 note describes real-estate security and names inconsistent with the core jewellery narrative; maturity disclosure is blank. Those terms are not imported into the model as verified debt schedules.\\n6. The secretary change was attributed to amended eligibility requirements, not a reported adverse resignation. A postal notice contains unrelated-looking “excipients” wording and a secretarial cover uses another scrip code, while the inner documents identify Retaggio. These are reliability warnings, not proof that all underlying business data are false.\\n\\nFull main audits, CARO and available IFC annexures from inception through FY26 were inspected, with separately retrieved original FY22–24 audit statements. Formal opinions were unmodified; no material uncertainty, adverse opinion or reported fraud finding was identified. That negative search does not resolve the explicit inconsistencies. No remuneration-to-cash comfort is inferred where the table itself conflicts.\\n\\n### Dated commitments versus observed delivery\\n\\n| Date | Commitment / proposal | Observed outcome by cutoff | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 22 March 2025 prospectus; 7 April 2025 listing | IPO net objects: ₹1,150 lakh working capital and ₹190 lakh debt repayment; gross issue 6,198,000 shares at ₹25. | Share issue/listing is corroborated in FY26 annual history. Deployment cannot be independently traced through a clean cash/premium bridge. | Share issuance delivered; economic deployment and cash returns unverified, not a completed-return success. Sources: prospectus, ar2026. |\\n| 11 December 2025 approval; 13–22 January 2026 issues | 19,110,000 warrants at ₹26, 25% upfront and 75% on optional exercise within 18 months. | March shares 18,168,160; 20 October 20,992,160. Signed 20 October notice confirms 504,000 new shares and ₹0.9828 crore received; 13,684,000 warrants remain. | Documented conversion milestones delivered; future ₹26.6838 crore is optional, not promised or committed cash. Sources: ar2026, allot_jun18, allot_aug13, allot_oct20. |\\n| 15 April 2026 proposal; 23 April 2026 incorporation reported subsequently | Proposed 76% investment in Lodha Heritage and transition of manufacturing/retail operations. | FY26 annual subsequent-event disclosure and Q1 consolidated review establish subsidiary existence. Original ownership/transition agreement and actual business transferred were not obtained. | Formation corroborated; 76% and business allocation remain explicitly qualified, no integration-success conclusion. Sources: ar2026, q1, subsidiary_secondary. |\\n| 15 April 2026 postal proposal, effective 1 April 2026 | MD annual salary proposed to rise from ₹24 lakh to ₹60 lakh, with additional perquisites capped at salary. | Retrieved notice states proposed terms; actual payments and final vote outcome not independently reconciled with the nil RPT table. | Proposal is not treated as actual remuneration paid or proven shareholder approval. Sources: postal, ar2026. |\\n| 26 August 2026 board approval; 16 October 2026 agreement | Purchase 3,261.65 sq ft property for ₹10 crore plus actual transaction charges. | Signed agreement announcement is available; instalments, paid amount, funding, readiness and incremental output are not established. | Agreement milestone delivered; commissioning, funding and returns remain open rather than assumed on time. Sources: property_bs. |\\n| FY26 controls assertions; 15 May 2026 audit | Board claims functioning audit trail and internal-audit arrangements. | Main audit/CARO contain contradictory audit-trail/internal-audit statements; separate IFC annexure also conflicts with exemption wording. | Control assertion is not independently validated; requires written reconciliation. Sources: ar2026, secretarial. |\\n\\n\\nThe table distinguishes a signed/allotted milestone from actual cash deployment, commissioned capacity or earned returns. It does not convert unverified outcomes into an EXEC score.\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\nAmounts below are **₹ lakh**, standalone, retaining source vintages rather than silently stitching them.\\n\\n| Period/vintage | Revenue | PAT | CFO reported | CFO tax-reclassified | Cash | Debt | AR |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY22_restated | 0.00 | -0.64 | -4.91 | -4.91 | 0.09 | 0.00 | 4.90 |\\n| FY23_restated | 2306.59 | 308.99 | -2189.12 | -2189.12 | 0.55 | 1089.07 | 1521.74 |\\n| FY24_restated | 2327.83 | 334.11 | 600.44 | 600.44 | 53.60 | 1140.20 | 406.06 |\\n| FY24_original | 2327.83 | 340.96 | 595.28 | 595.28 | 53.60 | 1140.20 | 406.06 |\\n| FY24_comparative_in_FY25 | 2327.83 | 340.96 | 658.08 | 591.87 | 53.60 | 1140.20 | 406.06 |\\n| FY25_original | 2349.21 | 243.28 | 68.06 | 25.13 | 1.27 | 1179.28 | 1502.54 |\\n| FY26_reported | 8398.45 | 894.67 | -2524.19 | -2524.19 | 0.94 | 1896.13 | 6489.42 |\\n\\n\\n| Period / vintage | EBITDA (₹ lakh) | EBIT (₹ lakh) | Cash after tax-reclassified CFO and all cash investing (₹ lakh) | Closing financing-side IC proxy (₹ lakh) |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY22_restated | -0.64 | -0.64 | -4.91 | 4.27 |\\n| FY23_restated | 415.91 | 414.30 | -2261.00 | 2615.46 |\\n| FY24_restated | 518.35 | 510.83 | 111.64 | 2944.23 |\\n| FY24_original | 517.87 | 510.35 | 106.48 | 2946.41 |\\n| FY24_comparative_in_FY25 | 517.87 | 510.35 | 103.07 | 2946.41 |\\n| FY25_original | 404.08 | 396.40 | 18.77 | 3281.14 |\\n| FY26_reported | 1201.30 | 1192.32 | -2530.45 | 8168.91 |\\n\\n\\nNormalized EBITDA/EBIT/PAT, reliable average-operating-capital ROIC/ROCE and average-owner-equity ROE remain NS: accounting scope, equity, tax and profit normalization are unresolved. The retained closing-capital calculations are labelled diagnostic proxies, not the framework’s average-capital ROIC. FY25 related advances and FY26 property/noncurrent balances require operating-versus-financing classification. Gross profit is not normalized from an unverified manufacturing/trading cost allocation. No separate contract-asset business was identified; lease obligations were not reliably quantified and are ND, not a claim that none can exist. Model operating expense assumptions do not certify lease completeness.\\n\\nReported FY24/FY25/FY26 EPS are approximately ₹3.64/₹2.60/₹4.92 in their respective statements. The FY26 denominator is unreliable as a weighted diluted measure. Full current dilution is shown separately; normalized diluted EPS is NS. FY26 standalone PAT has no subsidiary NCI deduction, whereas future subsidiary economics are explicitly conditional.\\n\\n### Earnings to cash and accounting vintages\\n\\nFY23's first operating year includes acquired balances and noncash takeover shares. The original cash-flow statement showed CFO approximately −₹18.5423 crore and included ₹2.97272 crore of other operating inflows, with issue-share cash ₹9.31816 crore. The next original report's FY23 comparative instead showed CFO −₹21.9943 crore; the prospectus restatement showed −₹21.8912 crore and share-issue cash ₹12.2909 crore. The prospectus identifies ₹5.2909 crore BTA share consideration. Without the acquired opening balances and a classification bridge, none is clean recurring operating cash.\\n\\nFY24 original CFO was ₹5.9528 crore after ₹0.6126 crore tax; the prospectus restatement says ₹6.0044 crore. FY25's FY24 comparative says ₹6.5808 crore and appears to put ₹0.6621 crore tax into financing, giving a separately labelled analytical operating figure ₹5.9187 crore. These differences are retained, not called interchangeable. FY25 reported CFO ₹0.6806 crore similarly becomes ₹0.2513 crore after reclassifying the apparent ₹0.4293 crore financing tax payment. The reclassification is analytical and needs confirmation of the underlying payments.\\n\\nFY26 reported CFO is **−₹25.2419 crore**, versus PAT ₹8.9467 crore. The operating reconciliation adds an unexplained **₹11.65 crore other inflow**. Removing it as a sensitivity produces −₹36.8919 crore, not a certified correction. It must not be automatically called share premium: disclosed premium additions are ₹13.4602 crore, so that proposed explanation does not itself reconcile.\\n\\nThe receivable/advance labels are unreliable but their aggregate reconciles: cash-flow outflows ₹37.6312 crore plus ₹11.3253 crore equal ₹48.9565 crore; balance-sheet AR increase ₹49.0307 crore less loan decrease ₹0.0742 crore is exactly the same. This report therefore does **not** falsely claim an aggregate working-capital mismatch. FY25 AR was regrouped upward by ₹0.8381 crore with a nearly offsetting advances reduction. Debt shifted ₹0.2453 crore between current/noncurrent without changing total debt.\\n\\nFY26 share capital ₹18.1682 crore + other equity ₹33.8386 crore + warrant money ₹10.7304 crore equals **₹62.7372 crore**. The printed equity subtotal ₹44.5690 crore omits share capital. Assets ₹88.5401 crore less liabilities ₹25.8029 crore also gives ₹62.7372 crore. This is an explicit arithmetic correction for diagnostics, not a signed amended balance sheet. The reported cash-flow movements net to −₹0.0032 crore versus cash balance change −₹0.0033 crore, a ₹0.0001 crore rounding residual, distinct from the material classification issues.\\n\\n### Working capital, returns and debt\\n\\nMarch receivables ₹64.8942 crore are about 282 closing-balance revenue-days. The note says invoice age under six months; that is not the same as contractual overdue age, and this report does not call them all delinquent. Average receivable days are lower because sales were concentrated in Q4. With only ₹0.0094 crore cash, collection timing matters more than a superficially modest debt/EBITDA ratio.\\n\\nDebt rose to ₹18.9613 crore; interest expense ₹1.3977 crore. Reported EBITDA ₹12.0130 crore implies apparently manageable leverage and cover, but cash realization and the maturity schedule remain unverified. FY26 funding cash included ₹7.1686 crore borrowings, ₹8.80 crore share-issue line and ₹10.7304 crore warrants, while issuance amounts/premium and the operating inflow do not provide a complete clean funding bridge. No unused credit facility is assumed.\\n\\nFY25–26 reported CFO totals −₹24.5613 crore versus PAT ₹11.3795 crore; tax-reclassified CFO is still weaker. Positive FY24 cash does not cure this. The cash score is low because of repeated collection dependence, not because every balance-sheet ambiguity is counted again. FY26's 15% current-tax rate is not extrapolated indefinitely: forward base uses 25%, with 15% and 30% sensitivities.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n### Price, share count and optional financing\\n\\nAt ₹52, 19 October issued shares 20,488,160 imply market capitalization ₹106.538432 crore. The next day's signed conversion notice confirms 504,000 shares and **₹0.9828 crore** remaining consideration received, taking issued shares to **20,992,160**. This event is inside the information cutoff but is not backdated into 19 October capitalization. Remaining warrants are 13,684,000; full conversion gives 34,676,160 shares and additional **₹26.6838 crore**, at ₹19.50 remaining cash per share. The already-paid 25% is not counted again.\\n\\nFull-diluted capitalization at the same stale ₹52 is ₹180.316032 crore, before credit for future exercise cash or changes in operations. It is not an economic enterprise value by itself. Exercise is the holder's option over 18 months from January 2026 allotment and is **not committed funding**. The old EPS of ₹4.92 uses a closing-share-like denominator and is not an adequate fully diluted forward earnings basis.\\n\\n### Current-share valuation cross-check\\n\\nAt the stale ₹52 reference and 19 October issued shares of 20,488,160, equity capitalization is ₹106.538432 crore. Dividing by FY26 reported standalone PAT ₹8.9467 crore gives **11.91× reported P/E**, purely diagnostic. Normalized P/E, current EV and current EV/normalized EBITDA are **NS**. The 20 October conversion is separately disclosed and is not silently backdated into this multiple.\\n\\nThis is a share-count-consistent arithmetic cross-check, not reliable normalized earnings power. Using the historical March debt and cash as though they were current would ignore subsequent conversion receipts, their deployment and the property obligation; therefore current EV, EV/normalized EBITDA and normalized P/E remain NS. Full-diluted shares and remaining exercise cash are shown separately, never mixed into a denominator without the associated cash/claims bridge. The 19 October price remains stale and non-executable.\\n\\n### Linked pre-entry and monthly cash model\\n\\n`model_inputs.json` declares each assumption; `model_output.json`/`canonical.json` retain every row. 223 requests, 221 unique complete or stopped paths, 97 failed paths retained. Source statements are not regenerated from arbitrary forecasts: the forward model is clearly separate from the historical extracts.\\n\\nThe starting point is the March balance sheet. Seven monthly/partial-month periods bridge to 19 October, using actual aggregate Q1 revenue divided across April–June, then explicitly assumed flat Q1 run rate through entry. No June balance sheet or October bank balance was available. AR, inventory and payables transition toward declared days; collection, purchases and supplier-payment identities are enforced. Known conversion receipts are credited only once, with the monthly pre-entry timing deliberately conservative. The last property-prepayment branch is charged before October operating receipts. **No optional rescue is allowed to cure any pre-entry breach.**\\n\\nFive forward years then calculate revenue, gross profit, operating expenses, WC, maintenance/expansion capex, depreciation, interest, taxes, principal and minority distributions. Customer receipts and supplier payments have front/back tranches, checked after every event. A path stops on its first cash-floor breach; no later operating projection, terminal windfall or ordinary IRR masks insolvency. Known 20 October conversion cash occurs before forward operating events; the post-entry property alternative settles immediately afterward, not after a month's collections.\\n\\nBase assumptions are 12% annual revenue growth from the Q1 annualized run-rate proxy, 12% EBITDA margin, AR 180 days, inventory 90 days and payables 25 days. Bear uses no growth, 7% margin, AR 450 days, inventory 180 days, payables 15 and an opening 20% receivable haircut. Bull uses 25% growth, 15% margin, AR 90 days, inventory 60 and payables 40. These are diagnostic choices, not probabilities or company guidance. Base tax is 25%, cash floor ₹0.005 crore, existing debt interest 10% and principal amortization 20% of original debt per year after entry, because a reliable actual schedule is missing. The ₹2.01 crore legacy tax settlement is an explicit pre-entry stress assumption.\\n\\nSeparate hypothetical rescue permits at most ₹5 crore gross new debt at 14% interest and 1% fee, then whole new shares at ₹26 with 3% issuance fee, always before the threatened cash event. All existing warrants remain reserved against 36 million authorized shares, leaving at most **1,323,840 rescue shares**, even if unexercised warrants later lapse. This conservative legal-headroom assumption must be verified. Debt and share caps, fees, new interest, amortization and dilution are cash-linked; the model never assumes an unlimited balancing plug. A rescue-labelled path is not a funded base case. Sub-1e-18-crore arithmetic residuals are ignored as numerical dust, never financed with an extra whole share.\\n\\n| Case / property / financing | Status | First breach | Year 5 price / IRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_False | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_True | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_False | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_True | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_False | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_True | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_False | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_True | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| base / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹31.77 / -10.11% |\\n| base / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹26.93 / -13.03% |\\n| base / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹31.77 / -10.11% |\\n| base / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹26.93 / -13.03% |\\n| base / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹31.76 / -10.11% |\\n| base / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹26.92 / -13.03% |\\n| base / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹31.76 / -10.11% |\\n| base / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹26.92 / -13.03% |\\n| bull / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹79.99 / 8.13% |\\n| bull / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹56.12 / 0.73% |\\n| bull / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹79.99 / 8.13% |\\n| bull / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹56.12 / 0.73% |\\n| bull / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹79.99 / 8.13% |\\n| bull / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹56.12 / 0.73% |\\n| bull / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹79.99 / 8.13% |\\n| bull / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹56.12 / 0.73% |\\n\\n\\n### Earned terminal value and valuation cross-check\\n\\nContinuation is earned only after five years' survival and a complete sixth-year cash test with **no new rescue**. Base perpetual growth is 4%, WACC 14%, with 0/2/6% growth and 12/16/18% WACC sensitivities. Year-six FCFF is NOPAT plus depreciation minus actual capex and change in operating working capital. Additional economic investment is required to keep the incremental return within an explicit 15% ROIC ceiling, with 10/12/20% sensitivities. Required reinvestment is the greater of NOPAT×growth/ROIC and incremental NOPAT/ROIC; any gap over physical reinvestment becomes real additional monthly capex. The engine reruns funding survival after that payment. A working-capital release cannot be capitalized as costless eternal growth.\\n\\nMinority economics are charged explicitly. Terminal enterprise value uses parent-attributable FCFF/(WACC−growth), then deducts all assumed parent debt. Cash added is limited to excess over the **entire next-year intraperiod reserve**, not merely a token floor. Distributing that excess must leave every continuation event at or above the floor. Existing and new operating property is not added again. A 6/8/10/12× parent EBIT enterprise-value cross-check is retained, with debt/cash handled consistently; it is not a free rerating assumption.\\n\\nEntry and exit execution costs are each 2%, with 0%/5% sensitivities. These are research haircuts, not observed spreads. Hurdle purchase prices at 12%, 15% and 18% require survival, terminal self-funding and dilution-aware proceeds. Even a surviving diagnostic IRR is not eligible for R because opening cash, true margins and current executability remain unresolved.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations and sensitivities\\n\\n| Financing mode | Hurdle | Feasible root interval |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| unfunded | 12.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| unfunded | 15.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| unfunded | 18.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| optional_rescue_and_warrants | 12.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| optional_rescue_and_warrants | 15.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| optional_rescue_and_warrants | 18.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n\\n\\nReverse analysis searches growth from −5% to +60% in one-percentage-point increments, separately with no optional finance and with bounded hypothetical debt/equity plus remaining-warrant exercise. Only adjacent feasible endpoints can start bisection. A failed interior point invalidates that bracket. All grid and refinement requests retain exact overrides and references to full cash paths; lossless deduplication shares identical effective paths rather than deleting failed trials. No root is extrapolated beyond the declared domain or across a cash discontinuity. In the base-margin/180-day-collection reverse grid, 25 unfunded and 34 optional-funded growth points survive; maximum conditional IRRs are still approximately −9.92% and −12.90%, respectively. Faster growth can reduce the return here because it consumes more receivable and inventory funding before generating distributable cash. These maxima are not forecasts.\\n\\nThe output also varies collection timing, AR/inventory/payable days, write-offs, margins, cash floor, property costs, tax, minority economics, debt amortization, rescue price/caps/fees, terminal growth/WACC/ROIC, multiples and execution costs, plus two-way growth/AR stress. A low P/E narrative cannot substitute for passing those cash requirements.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\n**Impairment-risk category: unresolved structural cash/funding and realizable-asset risk, not merely quotation volatility.** The decisive variable is the timing and recoverability of customer receivables relative to unavoidable cash obligations.\\n\\nThe strongest counterargument to this cautious conclusion is that reported operating PAT has grown, the formal audit opinion is unmodified, the invoice-age table reports balances under six months, and collecting the enlarged receivable book could release substantial cash. Documented conversions have brought cash, and further warrant exercise could help fund expansion. Those facts could support a better outcome if confirmed. They do not themselves reconcile the statements, prove bank availability, commit remaining warrant holders or establish an executable share price.\\n\\nThe principal loss mechanism is not merely a falling market multiple. It is customer cash arriving later or in smaller amounts than obligations require, while a major property purchase removes liquidity and warrant holders decline to provide the remaining cash. A modest-looking accounting profit can coexist with a funding failure.\\n\\nReceivable write-offs reduce realizable assets but do not pay suppliers or bank instalments. Rescue equity can dilute existing holders and may be legally unavailable at the assumed price/headroom; rescue debt adds fees, interest and maturities. The record therefore distinguishes: unsupported current survival (G3 Hold), specific failed diagnostic scenarios, and hypothetical rescued scenarios. It does not assert the company is already insolvent solely from a model.\\n\\nNo liquidation price is supported without confirmations of receivables, inventory quality, title, secured creditor priority and current obligations. Zero recovery is possible in a severe impairment path, but is not presented as a forecast probability. A cash-breached path carries NS ordinary IRR; a separately specified recovery analysis would be needed to estimate an impaired-holder outcome.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\nA fivefold ₹52-to-₹260 price outcome over five years requires roughly 38% annual price appreciation; tenfold to ₹520 requires roughly 58.5%. These are arithmetic hurdles, not targets. After all existing warrants, 34,676,160 shares would require equity values roughly ₹902 crore and ₹1,803 crore respectively, before any new rescue dilution.\\n\\nAt a hypothetical 10× normalized earnings valuation, that implies parent-attributable PAT about ₹90 crore or ₹180 crore. At 8% net margin, required revenue exceeds ₹1,100 crore or ₹2,200 crore, versus FY26 ₹83.98 crore and Q1's much smaller run rate. Additional minority leakage, financing costs, taxes and rescue dilution would raise the required operating outcome. A high exit multiple can reduce the needed earnings but introduces a separate assumption, not evidence.\\n\\nWithin the declared cash-linked cases and sensitivity domain, no surviving path reaches either 5× or 10× net proceeds. The strongest tested path is the high-growth, faster-collection bull case without further warrant exercise, roughly 8.13% annualized, or 1.48× net over five years. This bounded search is not a claim about every conceivable future; failed paths have no ordinary return and are not counted as zero-return survivors.\\n\\nThe central constraint is funding cumulative working capital and productive assets before those earnings arrive. No verified order book, volume trajectory, incremental-capital economics or demonstrated collections supports such a path. Capacity headroom and a property announcement are insufficient. **5×/10× classification: Speculative / unsupported.** It remains unproven, not impossible by decree and not an investable thesis.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline and date | Required milestone | Warning threshold | Thesis-break / gate consequence | Review evidence / timing |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Statement reconciliation | FY26 equity subtotal omits ₹18.1682 cr; CFO includes ₹11.65 cr unexplained inflow | Signed coherent equity, cash, premium and tax bridge | Any material difference remains unbridged in the next explanation | Continued G2 Hold; confirmed deliberate misstatement would require a separate G1 reassessment | Corrected issuer/auditor filing, immediately when published |\\n| Receivable collection | ₹64.8942 cr at 31 March 2026; about 282 closing revenue-days | Customer-confirmed subsequent cash receipts and due-date ageing sufficient to meet obligations | Collections fail the actual debt/property payment calendar, rather than an arbitrary DSO target | Confirmed unrecoverable balances or unavoidable unpaid obligations break a self-funded growth thesis | Next interim accounts, debtor confirmations and bank trail |\\n| Cash and debt maturity | March cash ₹0.0094 cr; debt ₹18.9613 cr; current headroom ND | Verified current cash plus committed facilities covers each dated obligation | Any event requires uncommitted warrant/equity/bank finance to remain solvent | An evidenced unavoidable shortfall would move G3 from Hold to funding failure | Bank cash, sanction/maturity documents and next financial filing |\\n| Property deployment | 16 October agreement ₹10 cr plus charges; payment/status ND | Verified instalments, funding, title, readiness and incremental operating plan | Cash paid or contractually due without matched available funding; capex does not produce the stated operating milestone | Documented payment default or uneconomic deployment that destroys recoverable equity | Payment/registration evidence and each subsequent operating update |\\n| Warrants and ownership claims | 20 October 20,992,160 shares; 13,684,000 warrants remain | Match each allotment to actual 75% receipt; clarify subsidiary ownership/transition | Reliance on optional proceeds before exercise, or unexplained share/NCI differences | Material undisclosed dilution/claims or a funding gap on non-exercise requires reassessment | Each allotment/lapse filing; exercise expiry 18 months after individual January 2026 issue |\\n| Controls and minority treatment | Audit-trail/IFC/CARO/RPT contradictions; proposed MD salary ₹60 lakh | Written reconciliation, actual remuneration/RPT ledger and corrected control disclosure | Repetition of material contradictions or undisclosed related transfers | Substantiated abuse/default, not an unverified suspicion, triggers governance/funding action | Auditor/secretarial responses, next annual/interim report |\\n| Execution and tradability | Capacity last evidenced FY24; subsidiary/property outcomes not verified; ₹52 last trade 14 October | Dated physical/customer milestones plus complete 20/60-session BSE liquidity and current restrictions | Announced milestones lack evidence; requested reference exit cannot be supported at 5% participation | A genuine suspension/effective inability to exit prevents new-purchase eligibility regardless of model value | Each operating update and before considering an actual trade; no monitoring automation created |\\n\\nNo arbitrary price alone clears these conditions. Downward price moves may improve hypothetical valuation while worsening liquidity or signalling missing information. Upward moves do not validate the financials.\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Hold — unresolved financial reliability, funding and tradability. No favorable investment classification and no implementation conclusion.** BQS, GQS and IAS remain NS; mathematical bounds are not rankings. The company is not established as a compounder or a funded turnaround, and severe wrongdoing has not been proven.\\n\\nThe next useful action is evidence repair, not an assumed purchase: obtain a coherent financial/cash/issuance reconciliation, validate current solvency and property funding, clarify subsidiary economics, then verify tradability. Only after those are resolved can a supported return model and price-dependent IAS be considered. This is research, not personalized financial advice or an instruction to transact.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\nPrimary issuer PDFs were downloaded and read; selected source facts, page locations and hashes are retained. Originals and OCR are deliberately excluded from the distributable package. Mirrors host original signed filings, not secondary rewritten financial tables. Secondary prices and the subsidiary-ownership summary are clearly labelled; they do not cure missing primary evidence.\\n\\n- [ar2022](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Annual-Report_2021-22.pdf): 37 pages;  image-only originals reviewed separately through audit 2022\\n- [ar2023](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Annual-Report_2022-23.pdf): 50 pages;  image-only originals reviewed separately through audit 2023\\n- [ar2024](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Annual-Report_2023-24.pdf): 43 pages;  audit and financial statements, original vintage\\n- [ar2025](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Annual-Report_2024-25.pdf): printed 36–44 audit/CARO/IFC; 45–47 financials; 48–63 notes\\n- [prospectus](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Prospectus.pdf): printed 29–34 risk; 70–75 objects; 99–115 business; F1–F18 financial/restatement; 195 debt\\n- [ar2026](https://cdn.financialreports.eu/financialreports/media/filings/59667/2026/RNS/59667_rns_2026-09-08_910e84ad-2545-4571-aa9a-0ebd89e40bbb.pdf): printed 55–63 audit/CARO/IFC; 64–66 statements; 80–98 notes; 17–24 board; 46–50 secretarial/remuneration\\n- [security](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz): 19 Oct 2026 official NSE interoperability; SM/ST, lot 3000, stale issued 18438160, not BSE price/trading history\\n- [q1](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/14082026/67f6f69b-0c14-4b2d-94c1-79c1920f9105.pdf): PDF 2–3 standalone review; 4 standalone results visually read; 5–7 consolidated review; 8 results\\n- [allot_oct20](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/20102026/a426f9a8-c13f-40c2-8ac3-cc3cffebf927.pdf): PDF 1–4: 504,000 shares,9828000 rupees received, total 20992160, 18-month exercise term\\n- [allot_aug13](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/13082026/abe44e17-4e7d-4a79-b6e8-fd545b263b05.pdf): PDF 1–4: 680,000 shares, total 20488160\\n- [allot_jun18](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/18062026/45b6067d-9c6d-4bda-9ec9-08adf5b9eda5.pdf): PDF 1–4: 272,000 shares, total 19808160\\n- [fy26_revised](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/24072026/ff4ae039-876d-4882-9a70-dd46441937db.pdf): 24 July resubmission in SME half-year format, unmodified opinion, not a reliable cash-flow reconciliation\\n- [secretarial](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/15052026/19b74446-8647-46e5-ab08-5ae197832a7c.pdf): entire report;  cover wrongly says 532911 but inner report names Retaggio;  no general compliance clearance inferred\\n- [postal](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/04052026/7c9ccec5-5071-4e05-8778-e6d9dd51b6ee.pdf): 15 April notice circulated 4 May; remuneration explanatory statement; excipients boilerplate not jewellery evidence\\n- [ar2026_bs](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/08092026/058454db-989e-4b1f-a2c8-170000142a1d.pdf): same issuer annual report, original BSE filing mirrored without rewriting\\n- [property_bs](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/16102026/f2a156c3-c697-4ea4-896d-697984693471.pdf): PDF 1–4: agreement dated 16 Oct 2026,10 crore plus actual charges,3261.65 sqft\\n- [audit2022](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/Auditors-Report_Financial-Statement_21-22.pdf): original full independent audit and annexures\\n- [audit2023](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/Audit-Report-and-Financial-Statement_22-23.pdf): original full independent audit and annexures\\n- [audit2024](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Financials-statements-for-FY-23-24.pdf): original full independent audit and annexures\\n- [market_iifl](https://www.indiainfoline.com/company/retaggio-industries-ltd-historical-data): last trade 14 Oct 2026 at 52;  no inferred 19 Oct closing trade\\n- [market_equitypandit](https://www.equitypandit.com/share-price/92685): last trade 14 Oct 2026 at 52;  no inferred 19 Oct closing trade\\n- [market_screener](https://www.screener.in/company/544391/): last trade 14 Oct 2026 at 52;  no inferred 19 Oct closing trade\\n- [subsidiary_secondary](https://au.marketscreener.com/news/retaggio-industries-limited-approves-incorporation-of-under-name-of-subsidiary-lodha-heritage-privat-ce7f59dadf8df725): 15 April 2026 announcement summary; 76% subscription; not assumed 100% owned\\n- [peer_sky25](https://skygold.co.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annual-Report_Sky-Gold-and-Diamonds-Limited-2024-25.pdf): PDF 3, 5, 7, 30; printed 58–59 financial highlights and printed 180 consolidated P&L\\n- [peer_rbz25](https://rbzjewellers.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annaul-Report-2024-2025.pdf): PDF 9–10 operating model; PDF 32 financial results; revised report 10 September 2025\\n- [peer_moksh25](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/AR_28914_MOKSH_2024_2025_A_2812467_08092025171608.pdf): PDF 56 main P&L; PDF 66 Note 22; PDF 16 board headline retained as a discrepancy\\n\\n`source_access_log.json` records current official-access outcomes, not a claim that unavailable bars are zero. `historical_inputs.json` preserves original/restated/comparative vintages and original-statement factual extracts. `audit_evidence.json` preserves contrary evidence and unresolved issues. `factor_evidence.json` supplies at least two facts, a contrary fact, source identifiers, confidence and rationale for every factor. `score_audit.json` gives exact contributions, caps and bounds. `model_inputs.json` contains every minimal forward assumption; no private helper, raw PDF, OCR, signed download URL or service identifier is required to rebuild.\\n\\nOpen questions include the complete cash-premium-issuance bridge, corrected audit/control text, current secured-loan terms and property payments, receivable confirmations and loss allowances, actual normalized tax, customer/product physical volumes, related-party cash and remuneration reconciliation, subsidiary ownership/transition, minority claims and current exchange execution conditions. Source publication and model assumptions are not interchangeable evidence.\\n\\n### Revision and final framework check\\n\\nOn 23 October 2026, the initial draft was revised to add the missing three named primary-sourced peers and explicit dated execution, current-share valuation, growth/addition, counterargument and monitoring presentations. Model assumptions, all factor scores, all four gate statuses and the thesis are unchanged. `framework_checklist.json` maps all 15 sections and identifies unavailable metrics rather than silently omitting them. Peer financials are selected FY25 facts, not claims of current peer quality.\\n\\nBoth compact and full backups are standalone. Guarded reconstruction removes only the declared generated outputs, including validation and output-hash attestations, then rebuilds from authored inputs and standard-library code. A baseline manifest retained outside the rebuild directory is compared afterward. Source hashes and successful reconstruction establish provenance and computational consistency, not truth of unresolved company claims.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"title\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\n**Hold: unresolved financial reliability, funding and tradability. BQS NS; GQS NS; IAS NS.** This is not a recommendation to buy at a low reported P/E. Retaggio's FY26 revenue and PAT expanded sharply, but cash realization, capital reconciliation and several internal contradictions prevent reliance on headline earnings. A formally unmodified audit is not the same as a clean investability assessment.\\n\\nRetaggio supplies jewellery, mainly to business customers. This is an unresolved speculative growth / capital-allocation situation, not an evidence-backed compounder.\\n\\nThe central economic question is whether the ₹64.89 crore March receivable balance is collectible, sufficiently quickly, without repeated outside funding. The second is whether the ₹10 crore October property agreement, plus charges, is supported by available cash and productive incremental returns. The third is how much profit and cash ultimately belongs to each listed share after warrants and the new subsidiary. None is answered by the nominal growth rate alone.\\n\\nEvidence of deliberate fraud, diversion or a severe governance violation has **not** been established. Missing evidence therefore creates holds and NS, not fabricated low scores or an automatic governance-Avoid label. The model is a conditional stress laboratory, not an asserted fair value. No ordinary return is shown for any cash-breached path.\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\n- Exact issuer: **Retaggio Industries Limited**, ISIN **INE0KWJ01014**, BSE SME **544391**, CIN L36990MH2022PLC374614. The fourth ISIN character is zero. Listed 7 April 2025.\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, India time**. Market observation cutoff: **19 October 2026**. Retrieval occurred 23 October, with later facts excluded.\\n- Reference ₹52 is a secondary **14 October last-traded price**, not a verified 19 October closing transaction or an executable quote. Do not infer a trade from the midpoint of the exchange security-master band.\\n- Diagnostic market capitalization: ₹106.538432 crore using the stale ₹52 reference and 20,488,160 shares outstanding on 19 October; it is not an executable current valuation.\\n- Scope: standalone through March 2026. June 2026 standalone results are separately retained; the limited-review filing also includes Lodha Heritage in consolidated scope. No artificial five-year consolidated series is created.\\n- FY22 is a dormant incorporation stub; FY23 includes a November business takeover. Only FY24–26 provide three complete fiscal observations, themselves affected by reporting-vintage and economic-comparability problems.\\n- Horizon and hurdles: five years; default 15% nominal annual equity hurdle before investor taxes. The terminal WACC is a separately declared 14% analytical assumption, not a measured cost of capital and not the same as the equity hurdle.\\n- Accounting basis: FY25 uses Indian GAAP Accounting Standards; FY26 describes Ind AS. A reliable transition/reconciliation was not established.\\n- Reference implementation test: ₹5 lakh, not the user's portfolio allocation. Official 19 October interoperability file shows a 3,000-share lot, but stale issued capital and encoded restrictions cannot replace a current BSE tradability check.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Required evidence to clear |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 Governance | Hold | Reconcile conflicting audit, capital, related-party and control disclosures; distinguish errors from misconduct. |\\n| G2 Financial reliability | Hold | Corrected equity total and cash/issuance bridge; reliable accounting-basis transition; reconciled audit trail, IFC and CARO findings. IAS NS and no quality ranking. |\\n| G3 Funding survival | Hold | Current bank cash, facilities and maturities; actual receivable collections; property payment schedule and committed funding. Scenario survival is not proof. |\\n| G4 Tradability | Hold | Complete official 20/60-session turnover, zero/no-trade days, spread, restrictions and executable lot/position capacity. |\\n\\nDocument confidence varies. Original financial statements and signed corporate-event details are primary facts, but several facts within them conflict. Current funding, true organic growth and forward earnings economics are Low confidence. Reported financial arithmetic is Medium-confidence transcription, not certification of underlying balances.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\n| Aggregate | Known contribution | Coverage | Raw bound | Effective bound | Final |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | 1.20 | 40% | 1.80–7.20 | 1.80–7.20 | NS |\\n| GQS | 1.00 | 30% | 1.70–8.00 | 1.70–5.00 | NS |\\n| IAS | 0.00 | 0% | 1.00–10.00 | 1.00–10.00 | NS |\\n\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Primary reason |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| GOV | NS | Low | Stewardship is not reliably scoreable before reconciliation; no substantiated severe-abuse override is asserted. |\\n| MOAT | 3 | Medium | Customer bargaining and replication risks are material; no verified durable pricing, brand or cost advantage. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | No defensible three- or five-year underlying comparable growth basis; nominal growth does not justify a starting score 9. |\\n| DUR | 4 | Medium | Some accessible runway exists, but unsupported customer conversion/expansion keeps this below the adequate band; returns are assessed separately. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | Additional-capital denominators and normalized earnings are unreliable; diagnostic ROIC is not a scoreable economic return. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Medium | Repeated weak realization in FY25–26 is not adequately explained as a temporary build; the GQS cap 5 applies, without replacing NS. |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | Current funding resilience is unscoreable; hypothetical surviving branches do not establish actual solvency. |\\n| EXEC | NS | Low | No reliable Medium-confidence delivery record exists for the enlarged operating scale. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | Normalized operating margins, tax and fully diluted per-share earnings are not established; headline PAT growth is not scoreable. |\\n| R | NS | Low | No supported return model or executable entry quote; illustrative returns cannot determine R. |\\n| D | NS | Low | Loss severity and actual funding resilience cannot be scored from conditional scenarios alone. |\\n| P | NS | Low | No supported price-implied-expectations score before the operating and funding base is established. |\\n| L | NS | Low | Liquidity and the ₹5 lakh reference exit test remain unscoreable; missing sessions are not zero trades. |\\n\\n\\nAll 13 canonical factors appear once. BQS uses GOV 20%, MOAT 20%, REV 12%, DUR 10%, CAP 12%, CASH 10%, BAL 8%, EXEC 8%. GQS uses REV 40%, DUR 20%, EARN 15%, CAP 15%, CASH 10%. IAS uses **unrounded final BQS** 25%, R 35%, D 20%, P 10%, L 10%. Decimal arithmetic and conventional half-up one-decimal presentation are used. No unknown weight is redistributed and no midpoint is invented.\\n\\nBQS known contribution is 0.20×3 + 0.10×4 + 0.10×2 = 1.20, missing weight 0.60, bound [1.80, 7.20]. GQS contribution is 0.20×4 + 0.10×2 = 1.00, missing weight 0.70, raw bound [1.70, 8.00]. The persistent weak-cash-realization override caps the computable upper outcome at 5, but **GQS stays NS**, not 5.0. FY23 cash contamination is not needed to trigger that override: FY25 conversion was weak and FY26 strongly negative even before questioning the positive unexplained inflow.\\n\\nIAS has 0% point-score coverage because Q is NS and all four investment factors are NS. Its prescribed missing-data bound is [1,10]; propagating BQS's narrower mathematical bound gives an additional conditional [1.20,9.30], not a score or confidence interval. A one-point MOAT change moves BQS's known contribution by 0.20; a one-point DUR change moves BQS by 0.10 and GQS by 0.20. These cannot repair missing evidence. No GOV≤3/G1-fail or G3-fail cap is asserted without adequate evidence. No ranking is permitted under the G2 hold.\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\nRetaggio sells gold and diamond jewellery, precious stones, other fancy jewellery and bullion, primarily B2B. The promoter's Vaibhav Gems business began in 2012 and was transferred under the November 2022 business-transfer arrangement. That history is useful operating context, not a listed-company five-year audited record. Shares issued as takeover consideration are capital employed, not free cash raised. The IPO issued 6,198,000 shares at ₹25, raising ₹15.495 crore gross; the prospectus allocated ₹11.50 crore to working capital and ₹1.90 crore to debt repayment, with ₹13.40 crore net objects. Those proceeds precede the March 2026 model anchor and are never added again.\\n\\nThe IPO business description emphasizes design, craftsmanship, customer orders and a factory at Options Primo, Mumbai. However, FY26 Note 24 classifies ₹83.8245 crore as traded goods and just ₹0.16 crore as labour receipts. Manufacturing narrative, purchase accounting and actual value added need reconciliation. Revenue is a poor standalone demand indicator when precious-metal prices and mix can move gross invoice value.\\n\\nThe prospectus reports FY24 capacity of 975,000 pieces, output 328,250, utilization 33.67%; September-half output 166,150 against 487,500 capacity, 34.08%. It lists 15 employees in March 2025; FY26 reports five permanent employees. No equivalent FY26 physical output, grams sold, outsourcing split, customer reorder cohorts or plant-level contribution margin was established. Fewer employees need not mean contraction in a trading/outsourcing model, but they do not verify scalable manufacturing either.\\n\\nLodha Heritage Private Limited was incorporated after March 2026. The April announcement summary specifies 76% subscription to ₹10 lakh initial capital and a proposed transition of manufacturing/retail operations. Original ownership confirmation is tracked separately; the Q1 original review confirms subsidiary scope, not wholly owned status. The diagnostic base assigns half the operations to that 76%-owned vehicle, hence a 12% economic minority interest, with 0%/24% sensitivities. This is an assumption, not an observed segment allocation.\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\nRelationships, design familiarity and the predecessor's operating history can help win repeat orders. They are not demonstrated switching costs, consumer brand pricing power, network effects or proprietary technology. The prospectus says there are no material customer contracts. FY26's credit-risk note reports five customers representing all revenue, although surrounding note inconsistencies require confirmation of that concentration.\\n\\nSmall workshop capacity and unused installed capacity mean supply is not obviously the constraint. Collection discipline, customer access, metal funding and mix are more important. Industry demand can grow while a small supplier earns little economic profit or finances its customers. This distinction keeps DUR separate from CAP. No peer multiple is used to imply a moat, and no national jewellery TAM is assigned to this issuer as accessible revenue. Prospectus risk 12 says jewellery designs are produced by job workers and are not registered under the Designs Act, leaving copying risk; this is stronger evidence against a proprietary-design moat than a generic industry assertion.\\n\\nCompetitive-position score 3 reflects contestable supply and weak contractual protection, not the audit problems already assigned to G2/GOV. Contrary evidence includes operating longevity and repeat-relationship potential; evidence of sustained excess returns or premium realizations is still missing.\\n\\n### Named economic peers, not a peer-average valuation\\n\\nThe peer window is deliberately fixed at FY25, a common completed period available before the cutoff. This is a structural business-model comparison, not a current trading screen or full forensic clearance of the peers. Stronger/weaker labels refer only to the specified channel, scale or reported margin dimension.\\n\\n| Peer / FY25 source | Primary operating evidence | Relative economic comparison | Comparability limits |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| [Sky Gold and Diamonds Limited](https://skygold.co.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annual-Report_Sky-Gold-and-Diamonds-Limited-2024-25.pdf) | B2B supply to jewellery retailers/wholesalers; disclosed capacity of 1,050 kg/month. FY25 consolidated revenue was ₹354,801.96 lakh and PAT ₹13,265.53 lakh; acquisitions of Sparkling Chains and Starmangalsutra expanded the product mix. | Stronger disclosed industrial scale and product/customer access than Retaggio; its manufacturing-supplier model is economically closer than a pure branded retailer. | Consolidated acquisition scope differs from Retaggio standalone. Reported PAT margin 3.74% illustrates that scale and metal-pass-through revenue do not guarantee high margins. No valuation multiple is transferred. PDF 3, 5, 7, 30; printed 58–59 financial highlights and printed 180 consolidated P&L. |\\n| [RBZ Jewellers Limited](https://rbzjewellers.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annaul-Report-2024-2025.pdf) | Integrated antique-gold manufacturing, wholesale/job work and Harit Zaveri retail. FY25 mix: wholesale 37% of revenue / 20% of volume; job work 2% / 50%; retail 61% / 30%. Revenue was ₹53,014.85 lakh and PAT before OCI ₹3,879.85 lakh. | Stronger disclosed direct-customer channel and mix visibility; the job-work volume/revenue disparity is a concrete warning against treating gross revenue as physical demand. | Retail integration and antique-gold specialization make its 7.32% PAT margin non-transferable to Retaggio. Not a like-for-like market-multiple or consolidated-margin comparison. PDF 9–10 operating model; PDF 32 financial results; revised report 10 September 2025. |\\n| [Moksh Ornaments Limited](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/AR_28914_MOKSH_2024_2025_A_2812467_08092025171608.pdf) | FY25 Note 22 separates domestic goods of ₹47,641.35 lakh, export sales ₹10,324.50 lakh and services ₹24.35 lakh. Main P&L revenue of ₹57,990.20 lakh and PAT ₹842.76 lakh imply a 1.45% PAT margin. | A weaker-margin comparator than RBZ and Sky on the same reported FY25 measure, despite substantial gross sales. It tests the assumption that jewellery-supply scale necessarily produces pricing power. | Different product/export mix, financing and accounting basis. The board headline of ₹58,251.51 lakh differs from main P&L/Note 22, so those identified statement values are used, not a blended number. No full peer-quality clearance is claimed. PDF 56 main P&L; PDF 66 Note 22; PDF 16 board headline retained as a discrepancy. |\\n\\n\\nRetaggio has not demonstrated Sky’s disclosed industrial scale or RBZ’s direct-customer channel. Moksh supplies a weaker-margin counterexample, not evidence that Retaggio deserves a premium: Retaggio’s own reported margin cannot establish superiority while G2 remains unresolved. Gold pass-through, product/export mix, job-work revenue recognition, acquisitions, retail integration and financing differ. No peer P/E, average margin or branded-retail multiple is transferred into Retaggio’s valuation.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\nFY23 restated revenue was ₹23.0659 crore; FY24 ₹23.2783 crore; FY25 ₹23.4921 crore; FY26 ₹83.9845 crore. FY26 nominal growth was about 257.5%. A three-interval FY23–26 CAGR can be calculated, but the first period contains a partial-year transfer and cannot establish organic demand. The FY24–26 two-interval CAGR is also only nominal. Five-year revenue CAGR is ND, not zero.\\n\\n| Nominal revenue interval | Start / end revenue (₹ crore) | Annual ₹ addition or total interval addition | Exact interval count | Growth / CAGR | Interpretation |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\\n| FY22–FY23 | 0 / 23.0659 | 23.0659 | 1 | NM | NM growth: dormant stub and partial business takeover |\\n| FY23–FY24 | 23.0659 / 23.2783 | 0.2124 | 1 | 0.92% | Nominal only; partial-year acquisition base |\\n| FY24–FY25 | 23.2783 / 23.4921 | 0.2138 | 1 | 0.92% | Nominal reported revenue, not price/volume growth |\\n| FY25–FY26 | 23.4921 / 83.9845 | 60.4924 | 1 | 257.50% | Nominal reported revenue, major pass-through/mix uncertainty |\\n| FY24–FY26 | 23.2783 / 83.9845 | 60.7062 | 2 | 89.94% | Two-year CAGR, not the preferred comparable organic window |\\n| FY23–FY26 | 23.0659 / 83.9845 | 60.9186 | 3 | 53.84% | Three-year arithmetic CAGR only; partial takeover base |\\n| Three-year / five-year organic | Not established | Not established | 3 / 5 | NS / NS | No comparable volume/constant-price, acquisition-adjusted series; no invented endpoints. |\\n\\n\\nThe product mix changed materially: FY24 prospectus revenue included gold 24KT ₹3.0329 crore, 18KT ₹6.1738 crore, 22KT ₹5.2710 crore and diamond jewellery ₹8.8006 crore. H1 FY25 reported 18KT ₹3.3446 crore, diamonds ₹5.6141 crore and job work ₹0.0702 crore. No price/volume bridge reconciles these with FY26's traded-goods surge. Geographic percentages also conflict between prospectus risk and business sections; the report does not select the more attractive version.\\n\\nQ1 FY27 original standalone results: revenue ₹13.1518 crore, EBITDA ₹1.6504 crore, interest ₹0.1806 crore, depreciation ₹0.0216 crore, PBT ₹1.4482 crore, tax ₹0.2172 crore and PAT ₹1.2310 crore. Q1 FY26 comparator revenue was ₹2.6720 crore and PAT a loss of ₹0.1386 crore. Q4 FY26 revenue was ₹51.4367 crore: a very large portion of annual sales arrived late in the year, consistent with high year-end receivables. Q1 is not annualized as a supported forecast and does not earn a trajectory-score upgrade.\\n\\nREV remains NS under the framework's explicit gross-price/pass-through and acquisition-comparability rule. A naïve ≥30% growth starting score of 9 would be inappropriate. The comparison uses declared periods rather than shopping for the highest CAGR.\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nThe accessible runway depends on customers paying, incremental orders carrying adequate contribution and the new property producing more than its cost of capital. Capacity headroom alone is insufficient. IPO proceeds and subsequent warrants have expanded financial capital much faster than the audited physical asset base.\\n\\nThe 16 October property agreement specifies ₹10 crore consideration plus stamp duty, registration and other actual charges for 3,261.65 square feet from M S Mody Builders and Developers. Management says it is not a related-party transaction. Payment instalments, amount already paid, unit economics, incremental production and rental alternatives were not disclosed in the retrieved notice. The model tests payment before entry versus immediately after the known 20 October conversion, with assumed ₹0.7 crore charges and ₹0/₹1 crore charge sensitivities. It does not invent a paid balance or treat the property as simultaneously productive operating capital and a separate terminal windfall.\\n\\nExisting ₹3.2 crore noncurrent property receives no incremental recovery value in the core model because title, liquidity, rental income and independent valuation are unverified. A future verified recovery can be a separately funded scenario, not a concealed valuation plug. Capitalizing business-transfer consideration and recognizing diluted shares is necessary even when no current cash was paid.\\n\\nCAP is NS. Diagnostic financing-side invested capital equals arithmetic equity plus debt less cash, including warrant money and noncash takeover capital. It is not clean operating invested capital: advances/property classifications, current liabilities, original-vs-restated reserves and the FY26 equity error matter. NOPAT at an assumed 25% tax is a transparent proxy, not normalized reported profit. Three-/five-year economic incremental ROIC is NS; the one-year numerator/denominator bridge is retained for inspection rather than elevated into a score.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\nSavinay Lodha is managing director and became CFO after the December 2025 CFO resignation, concentrating management and financial responsibility. Original reports disclose the promoter business transfer and noncash director transactions; the historical CARO states Section 192 compliance. These facts require scrutiny but do not by themselves establish misconduct.\\n\\nThe April 2026 postal notice proposed increasing the managing director’s salary from ₹24 lakh to ₹60 lakh annually, effective 1 April 2026, with perquisites limited to an equal amount. This is a proposed authorization in the retrieved notice, not proof of actual cash paid or an independently verified vote outcome. The salary alone is about 6.7% of reported FY26 PAT, versus roughly 239% of FY25 tax-reclassified CFO; potential perquisites are additional. The FY26 board ratio reports 8:1 remuneration to median employee pay while the RPT table says nil managerial remuneration, so a clean five-year remuneration comparison cannot be asserted.\\n\\nAt IPO, the issuer disclosed four company tax matters totalling ₹85.68 lakh and 12 promoter/director matters totalling ₹359.17 lakh. Director amounts are not automatically company liabilities. The prospectus also describes pending registration of the promoter-owned office transfer and factory-license/registration formalities. These are title/compliance questions to close, not established misconduct.\\n\\nMaterial issues requiring written reconciliation:\\n\\n1. FY26 board audit-trail assurance contradicts the main audit's statement that audit-trail software was not used/operational throughout. Main audit says IFC reporting is exempt; a separate annexure nevertheless gives a positive IFC opinion. The latter contradiction also appears in FY25.\\n2. FY26 CARO says no IPO and no preferential/private placement despite the April 2025 IPO and January/March 2026 warrant transactions. A no-working-capital-limit-above-₹5-crore clause is difficult to reconcile with the earlier ₹7 crore cash-credit limit, but outstanding bank debt alone is not proof of a current sanctioned limit.\\n3. Authorized-share and warrant-note figures conflict with board resolutions and signed allotment notices. Current dilution uses the latter, while legal rescue headroom remains conditional on confirmation.\\n4. RPT aggregate tables showing nil loans/remuneration conflict with ₹5.48 lakh related loans and other remuneration disclosures. FY25 related advances and a promoter receivable need a complete cash/terms/repayment ledger, not an assumed zero exposure.\\n5. The FY26 note describes real-estate security and names inconsistent with the core jewellery narrative; maturity disclosure is blank. Those terms are not imported into the model as verified debt schedules.\\n6. The secretary change was attributed to amended eligibility requirements, not a reported adverse resignation. A postal notice contains unrelated-looking “excipients” wording and a secretarial cover uses another scrip code, while the inner documents identify Retaggio. These are reliability warnings, not proof that all underlying business data are false.\\n\\nFull main audits, CARO and available IFC annexures from inception through FY26 were inspected, with separately retrieved original FY22–24 audit statements. Formal opinions were unmodified; no material uncertainty, adverse opinion or reported fraud finding was identified. That negative search does not resolve the explicit inconsistencies. No remuneration-to-cash comfort is inferred where the table itself conflicts.\\n\\n### Dated commitments versus observed delivery\\n\\n| Date | Commitment / proposal | Observed outcome by cutoff | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 22 March 2025 prospectus; 7 April 2025 listing | IPO net objects: ₹1,150 lakh working capital and ₹190 lakh debt repayment; gross issue 6,198,000 shares at ₹25. | Share issue/listing is corroborated in FY26 annual history. Deployment cannot be independently traced through a clean cash/premium bridge. | Share issuance delivered; economic deployment and cash returns unverified, not a completed-return success. Sources: prospectus, ar2026. |\\n| 11 December 2025 approval; 13–22 January 2026 issues | 19,110,000 warrants at ₹26, 25% upfront and 75% on optional exercise within 18 months. | March shares 18,168,160; 20 October 20,992,160. Signed 20 October notice confirms 504,000 new shares and ₹0.9828 crore received; 13,684,000 warrants remain. | Documented conversion milestones delivered; future ₹26.6838 crore is optional, not promised or committed cash. Sources: ar2026, allot_jun18, allot_aug13, allot_oct20. |\\n| 15 April 2026 proposal; 23 April 2026 incorporation reported subsequently | Proposed 76% investment in Lodha Heritage and transition of manufacturing/retail operations. | FY26 annual subsequent-event disclosure and Q1 consolidated review establish subsidiary existence. Original ownership/transition agreement and actual business transferred were not obtained. | Formation corroborated; 76% and business allocation remain explicitly qualified, no integration-success conclusion. Sources: ar2026, q1, subsidiary_secondary. |\\n| 15 April 2026 postal proposal, effective 1 April 2026 | MD annual salary proposed to rise from ₹24 lakh to ₹60 lakh, with additional perquisites capped at salary. | Retrieved notice states proposed terms; actual payments and final vote outcome not independently reconciled with the nil RPT table. | Proposal is not treated as actual remuneration paid or proven shareholder approval. Sources: postal, ar2026. |\\n| 26 August 2026 board approval; 16 October 2026 agreement | Purchase 3,261.65 sq ft property for ₹10 crore plus actual transaction charges. | Signed agreement announcement is available; instalments, paid amount, funding, readiness and incremental output are not established. | Agreement milestone delivered; commissioning, funding and returns remain open rather than assumed on time. Sources: property_bs. |\\n| FY26 controls assertions; 15 May 2026 audit | Board claims functioning audit trail and internal-audit arrangements. | Main audit/CARO contain contradictory audit-trail/internal-audit statements; separate IFC annexure also conflicts with exemption wording. | Control assertion is not independently validated; requires written reconciliation. Sources: ar2026, secretarial. |\\n\\n\\nThe table distinguishes a signed/allotted milestone from actual cash deployment, commissioned capacity or earned returns. It does not convert unverified outcomes into an EXEC score.\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\nAmounts below are **₹ lakh**, standalone, retaining source vintages rather than silently stitching them.\\n\\n| Period/vintage | Revenue | PAT | CFO reported | CFO tax-reclassified | Cash | Debt | AR |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY22_restated | 0.00 | -0.64 | -4.91 | -4.91 | 0.09 | 0.00 | 4.90 |\\n| FY23_restated | 2306.59 | 308.99 | -2189.12 | -2189.12 | 0.55 | 1089.07 | 1521.74 |\\n| FY24_restated | 2327.83 | 334.11 | 600.44 | 600.44 | 53.60 | 1140.20 | 406.06 |\\n| FY24_original | 2327.83 | 340.96 | 595.28 | 595.28 | 53.60 | 1140.20 | 406.06 |\\n| FY24_comparative_in_FY25 | 2327.83 | 340.96 | 658.08 | 591.87 | 53.60 | 1140.20 | 406.06 |\\n| FY25_original | 2349.21 | 243.28 | 68.06 | 25.13 | 1.27 | 1179.28 | 1502.54 |\\n| FY26_reported | 8398.45 | 894.67 | -2524.19 | -2524.19 | 0.94 | 1896.13 | 6489.42 |\\n\\n\\n| Period / vintage | EBITDA (₹ lakh) | EBIT (₹ lakh) | Cash after tax-reclassified CFO and all cash investing (₹ lakh) | Closing financing-side IC proxy (₹ lakh) |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY22_restated | -0.64 | -0.64 | -4.91 | 4.27 |\\n| FY23_restated | 415.91 | 414.30 | -2261.00 | 2615.46 |\\n| FY24_restated | 518.35 | 510.83 | 111.64 | 2944.23 |\\n| FY24_original | 517.87 | 510.35 | 106.48 | 2946.41 |\\n| FY24_comparative_in_FY25 | 517.87 | 510.35 | 103.07 | 2946.41 |\\n| FY25_original | 404.08 | 396.40 | 18.77 | 3281.14 |\\n| FY26_reported | 1201.30 | 1192.32 | -2530.45 | 8168.91 |\\n\\n\\nNormalized EBITDA/EBIT/PAT, reliable average-operating-capital ROIC/ROCE and average-owner-equity ROE remain NS: accounting scope, equity, tax and profit normalization are unresolved. The retained closing-capital calculations are labelled diagnostic proxies, not the framework’s average-capital ROIC. FY25 related advances and FY26 property/noncurrent balances require operating-versus-financing classification. Gross profit is not normalized from an unverified manufacturing/trading cost allocation. No separate contract-asset business was identified; lease obligations were not reliably quantified and are ND, not a claim that none can exist. Model operating expense assumptions do not certify lease completeness.\\n\\nReported FY24/FY25/FY26 EPS are approximately ₹3.64/₹2.60/₹4.92 in their respective statements. The FY26 denominator is unreliable as a weighted diluted measure. Full current dilution is shown separately; normalized diluted EPS is NS. FY26 standalone PAT has no subsidiary NCI deduction, whereas future subsidiary economics are explicitly conditional.\\n\\n### Earnings to cash and accounting vintages\\n\\nFY23's first operating year includes acquired balances and noncash takeover shares. The original cash-flow statement showed CFO approximately −₹18.5423 crore and included ₹2.97272 crore of other operating inflows, with issue-share cash ₹9.31816 crore. The next original report's FY23 comparative instead showed CFO −₹21.9943 crore; the prospectus restatement showed −₹21.8912 crore and share-issue cash ₹12.2909 crore. The prospectus identifies ₹5.2909 crore BTA share consideration. Without the acquired opening balances and a classification bridge, none is clean recurring operating cash.\\n\\nFY24 original CFO was ₹5.9528 crore after ₹0.6126 crore tax; the prospectus restatement says ₹6.0044 crore. FY25's FY24 comparative says ₹6.5808 crore and appears to put ₹0.6621 crore tax into financing, giving a separately labelled analytical operating figure ₹5.9187 crore. These differences are retained, not called interchangeable. FY25 reported CFO ₹0.6806 crore similarly becomes ₹0.2513 crore after reclassifying the apparent ₹0.4293 crore financing tax payment. The reclassification is analytical and needs confirmation of the underlying payments.\\n\\nFY26 reported CFO is **−₹25.2419 crore**, versus PAT ₹8.9467 crore. The operating reconciliation adds an unexplained **₹11.65 crore other inflow**. Removing it as a sensitivity produces −₹36.8919 crore, not a certified correction. It must not be automatically called share premium: disclosed premium additions are ₹13.4602 crore, so that proposed explanation does not itself reconcile.\\n\\nThe receivable/advance labels are unreliable but their aggregate reconciles: cash-flow outflows ₹37.6312 crore plus ₹11.3253 crore equal ₹48.9565 crore; balance-sheet AR increase ₹49.0307 crore less loan decrease ₹0.0742 crore is exactly the same. This report therefore does **not** falsely claim an aggregate working-capital mismatch. FY25 AR was regrouped upward by ₹0.8381 crore with a nearly offsetting advances reduction. Debt shifted ₹0.2453 crore between current/noncurrent without changing total debt.\\n\\nFY26 share capital ₹18.1682 crore + other equity ₹33.8386 crore + warrant money ₹10.7304 crore equals **₹62.7372 crore**. The printed equity subtotal ₹44.5690 crore omits share capital. Assets ₹88.5401 crore less liabilities ₹25.8029 crore also gives ₹62.7372 crore. This is an explicit arithmetic correction for diagnostics, not a signed amended balance sheet. The reported cash-flow movements net to −₹0.0032 crore versus cash balance change −₹0.0033 crore, a ₹0.0001 crore rounding residual, distinct from the material classification issues.\\n\\n### Working capital, returns and debt\\n\\nMarch receivables ₹64.8942 crore are about 282 closing-balance revenue-days. The note says invoice age under six months; that is not the same as contractual overdue age, and this report does not call them all delinquent. Average receivable days are lower because sales were concentrated in Q4. With only ₹0.0094 crore cash, collection timing matters more than a superficially modest debt/EBITDA ratio.\\n\\nDebt rose to ₹18.9613 crore; interest expense ₹1.3977 crore. Reported EBITDA ₹12.0130 crore implies apparently manageable leverage and cover, but cash realization and the maturity schedule remain unverified. FY26 funding cash included ₹7.1686 crore borrowings, ₹8.80 crore share-issue line and ₹10.7304 crore warrants, while issuance amounts/premium and the operating inflow do not provide a complete clean funding bridge. No unused credit facility is assumed.\\n\\nFY25–26 reported CFO totals −₹24.5613 crore versus PAT ₹11.3795 crore; tax-reclassified CFO is still weaker. Positive FY24 cash does not cure this. The cash score is low because of repeated collection dependence, not because every balance-sheet ambiguity is counted again. FY26's 15% current-tax rate is not extrapolated indefinitely: forward base uses 25%, with 15% and 30% sensitivities.\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\n### Price, share count and optional financing\\n\\nAt ₹52, 19 October issued shares 20,488,160 imply market capitalization ₹106.538432 crore. The next day's signed conversion notice confirms 504,000 shares and **₹0.9828 crore** remaining consideration received, taking issued shares to **20,992,160**. This event is inside the information cutoff but is not backdated into 19 October capitalization. Remaining warrants are 13,684,000; full conversion gives 34,676,160 shares and additional **₹26.6838 crore**, at ₹19.50 remaining cash per share. The already-paid 25% is not counted again.\\n\\nFull-diluted capitalization at the same stale ₹52 is ₹180.316032 crore, before credit for future exercise cash or changes in operations. It is not an economic enterprise value by itself. Exercise is the holder's option over 18 months from January 2026 allotment and is **not committed funding**. The old EPS of ₹4.92 uses a closing-share-like denominator and is not an adequate fully diluted forward earnings basis.\\n\\n### Current-share valuation cross-check\\n\\nAt the stale ₹52 reference and 19 October issued shares of 20,488,160, equity capitalization is ₹106.538432 crore. Dividing by FY26 reported standalone PAT ₹8.9467 crore gives **11.91× reported P/E**, purely diagnostic. Normalized P/E, current EV and current EV/normalized EBITDA are **NS**. The 20 October conversion is separately disclosed and is not silently backdated into this multiple.\\n\\nThis is a share-count-consistent arithmetic cross-check, not reliable normalized earnings power. Using the historical March debt and cash as though they were current would ignore subsequent conversion receipts, their deployment and the property obligation; therefore current EV, EV/normalized EBITDA and normalized P/E remain NS. Full-diluted shares and remaining exercise cash are shown separately, never mixed into a denominator without the associated cash/claims bridge. The 19 October price remains stale and non-executable.\\n\\n### Linked pre-entry and monthly cash model\\n\\n`model_inputs.json` declares each assumption; `model_output.json`/`canonical.json` retain every row. 223 requests, 221 unique complete or stopped paths, 97 failed paths retained. Source statements are not regenerated from arbitrary forecasts: the forward model is clearly separate from the historical extracts.\\n\\nThe starting point is the March balance sheet. Seven monthly/partial-month periods bridge to 19 October, using actual aggregate Q1 revenue divided across April–June, then explicitly assumed flat Q1 run rate through entry. No June balance sheet or October bank balance was available. AR, inventory and payables transition toward declared days; collection, purchases and supplier-payment identities are enforced. Known conversion receipts are credited only once, with the monthly pre-entry timing deliberately conservative. The last property-prepayment branch is charged before October operating receipts. **No optional rescue is allowed to cure any pre-entry breach.**\\n\\nFive forward years then calculate revenue, gross profit, operating expenses, WC, maintenance/expansion capex, depreciation, interest, taxes, principal and minority distributions. Customer receipts and supplier payments have front/back tranches, checked after every event. A path stops on its first cash-floor breach; no later operating projection, terminal windfall or ordinary IRR masks insolvency. Known 20 October conversion cash occurs before forward operating events; the post-entry property alternative settles immediately afterward, not after a month's collections.\\n\\nBase assumptions are 12% annual revenue growth from the Q1 annualized run-rate proxy, 12% EBITDA margin, AR 180 days, inventory 90 days and payables 25 days. Bear uses no growth, 7% margin, AR 450 days, inventory 180 days, payables 15 and an opening 20% receivable haircut. Bull uses 25% growth, 15% margin, AR 90 days, inventory 60 and payables 40. These are diagnostic choices, not probabilities or company guidance. Base tax is 25%, cash floor ₹0.005 crore, existing debt interest 10% and principal amortization 20% of original debt per year after entry, because a reliable actual schedule is missing. The ₹2.01 crore legacy tax settlement is an explicit pre-entry stress assumption.\\n\\nSeparate hypothetical rescue permits at most ₹5 crore gross new debt at 14% interest and 1% fee, then whole new shares at ₹26 with 3% issuance fee, always before the threatened cash event. All existing warrants remain reserved against 36 million authorized shares, leaving at most **1,323,840 rescue shares**, even if unexercised warrants later lapse. This conservative legal-headroom assumption must be verified. Debt and share caps, fees, new interest, amortization and dilution are cash-linked; the model never assumes an unlimited balancing plug. A rescue-labelled path is not a funded base case. Sub-1e-18-crore arithmetic residuals are ignored as numerical dust, never financed with an extra whole share.\\n\\n| Case / property / financing | Status | First breach | Year 5 price / IRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| bear / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_False | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_True | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_False | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_True | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_False | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_True | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_False | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| bear / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_True | FAILED_PRE | pre1: front_supplier_payments; ₹2.23cr | NS; no ordinary return after breach |\\n| base / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹31.77 / -10.11% |\\n| base / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹26.93 / -13.03% |\\n| base / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹31.77 / -10.11% |\\n| base / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹26.93 / -13.03% |\\n| base / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹31.76 / -10.11% |\\n| base / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹26.92 / -13.03% |\\n| base / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹31.76 / -10.11% |\\n| base / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹26.92 / -13.03% |\\n| bull / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹79.99 / 8.13% |\\n| bull / property_pre / rescue_False / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹56.12 / 0.73% |\\n| bull / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹79.99 / 8.13% |\\n| bull / property_pre / rescue_True / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹56.12 / 0.73% |\\n| bull / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹79.99 / 8.13% |\\n| bull / property_post / rescue_False / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹56.12 / 0.73% |\\n| bull / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_False | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹79.99 / 8.13% |\\n| bull / property_post / rescue_True / warrants_True | SURVIVES_DIAGNOSTIC_ONLY | none | ₹56.12 / 0.73% |\\n\\n\\n### Earned terminal value and valuation cross-check\\n\\nContinuation is earned only after five years' survival and a complete sixth-year cash test with **no new rescue**. Base perpetual growth is 4%, WACC 14%, with 0/2/6% growth and 12/16/18% WACC sensitivities. Year-six FCFF is NOPAT plus depreciation minus actual capex and change in operating working capital. Additional economic investment is required to keep the incremental return within an explicit 15% ROIC ceiling, with 10/12/20% sensitivities. Required reinvestment is the greater of NOPAT×growth/ROIC and incremental NOPAT/ROIC; any gap over physical reinvestment becomes real additional monthly capex. The engine reruns funding survival after that payment. A working-capital release cannot be capitalized as costless eternal growth.\\n\\nMinority economics are charged explicitly. Terminal enterprise value uses parent-attributable FCFF/(WACC−growth), then deducts all assumed parent debt. Cash added is limited to excess over the **entire next-year intraperiod reserve**, not merely a token floor. Distributing that excess must leave every continuation event at or above the floor. Existing and new operating property is not added again. A 6/8/10/12× parent EBIT enterprise-value cross-check is retained, with debt/cash handled consistently; it is not a free rerating assumption.\\n\\nEntry and exit execution costs are each 2%, with 0%/5% sensitivities. These are research haircuts, not observed spreads. Hurdle purchase prices at 12%, 15% and 18% require survival, terminal self-funding and dilution-aware proceeds. Even a surviving diagnostic IRR is not eligible for R because opening cash, true margins and current executability remain unresolved.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations and sensitivities\\n\\n| Financing mode | Hurdle | Feasible root interval |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| unfunded | 12.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| unfunded | 15.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| unfunded | 18.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| optional_rescue_and_warrants | 12.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| optional_rescue_and_warrants | 15.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n| optional_rescue_and_warrants | 18.00% | NO_FEASIBLE_ROOT_IN_DECLARED_GRID |\\n\\n\\nReverse analysis searches growth from −5% to +60% in one-percentage-point increments, separately with no optional finance and with bounded hypothetical debt/equity plus remaining-warrant exercise. Only adjacent feasible endpoints can start bisection. A failed interior point invalidates that bracket. All grid and refinement requests retain exact overrides and references to full cash paths; lossless deduplication shares identical effective paths rather than deleting failed trials. No root is extrapolated beyond the declared domain or across a cash discontinuity. In the base-margin/180-day-collection reverse grid, 25 unfunded and 34 optional-funded growth points survive; maximum conditional IRRs are still approximately −9.92% and −12.90%, respectively. Faster growth can reduce the return here because it consumes more receivable and inventory funding before generating distributable cash. These maxima are not forecasts.\\n\\nThe output also varies collection timing, AR/inventory/payable days, write-offs, margins, cash floor, property costs, tax, minority economics, debt amortization, rescue price/caps/fees, terminal growth/WACC/ROIC, multiples and execution costs, plus two-way growth/AR stress. A low P/E narrative cannot substitute for passing those cash requirements.\\n\\n\"","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\n**Impairment-risk category: unresolved structural cash/funding and realizable-asset risk, not merely quotation volatility.** The decisive variable is the timing and recoverability of customer receivables relative to unavoidable cash obligations.\\n\\nThe strongest counterargument to this cautious conclusion is that reported operating PAT has grown, the formal audit opinion is unmodified, the invoice-age table reports balances under six months, and collecting the enlarged receivable book could release substantial cash. Documented conversions have brought cash, and further warrant exercise could help fund expansion. Those facts could support a better outcome if confirmed. They do not themselves reconcile the statements, prove bank availability, commit remaining warrant holders or establish an executable share price.\\n\\nThe principal loss mechanism is not merely a falling market multiple. It is customer cash arriving later or in smaller amounts than obligations require, while a major property purchase removes liquidity and warrant holders decline to provide the remaining cash. A modest-looking accounting profit can coexist with a funding failure.\\n\\nReceivable write-offs reduce realizable assets but do not pay suppliers or bank instalments. Rescue equity can dilute existing holders and may be legally unavailable at the assumed price/headroom; rescue debt adds fees, interest and maturities. The record therefore distinguishes: unsupported current survival (G3 Hold), specific failed diagnostic scenarios, and hypothetical rescued scenarios. It does not assert the company is already insolvent solely from a model.\\n\\nNo liquidation price is supported without confirmations of receivables, inventory quality, title, secured creditor priority and current obligations. Zero recovery is possible in a severe impairment path, but is not presented as a forecast probability. A cash-breached path carries NS ordinary IRR; a separately specified recovery analysis would be needed to estimate an impaired-holder outcome.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\nA fivefold ₹52-to-₹260 price outcome over five years requires roughly 38% annual price appreciation; tenfold to ₹520 requires roughly 58.5%. These are arithmetic hurdles, not targets. After all existing warrants, 34,676,160 shares would require equity values roughly ₹902 crore and ₹1,803 crore respectively, before any new rescue dilution.\\n\\nAt a hypothetical 10× normalized earnings valuation, that implies parent-attributable PAT about ₹90 crore or ₹180 crore. At 8% net margin, required revenue exceeds ₹1,100 crore or ₹2,200 crore, versus FY26 ₹83.98 crore and Q1's much smaller run rate. Additional minority leakage, financing costs, taxes and rescue dilution would raise the required operating outcome. A high exit multiple can reduce the needed earnings but introduces a separate assumption, not evidence.\\n\\nWithin the declared cash-linked cases and sensitivity domain, no surviving path reaches either 5× or 10× net proceeds. The strongest tested path is the high-growth, faster-collection bull case without further warrant exercise, roughly 8.13% annualized, or 1.48× net over five years. This bounded search is not a claim about every conceivable future; failed paths have no ordinary return and are not counted as zero-return survivors.\\n\\nThe central constraint is funding cumulative working capital and productive assets before those earnings arrive. No verified order book, volume trajectory, incremental-capital economics or demonstrated collections supports such a path. Capacity headroom and a property announcement are insufficient. **5×/10× classification: Speculative / unsupported.** It remains unproven, not impossible by decree and not an investable thesis.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\n| KPI / event | Baseline and date | Required milestone | Warning threshold | Thesis-break / gate consequence | Review evidence / timing |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Statement reconciliation | FY26 equity subtotal omits ₹18.1682 cr; CFO includes ₹11.65 cr unexplained inflow | Signed coherent equity, cash, premium and tax bridge | Any material difference remains unbridged in the next explanation | Continued G2 Hold; confirmed deliberate misstatement would require a separate G1 reassessment | Corrected issuer/auditor filing, immediately when published |\\n| Receivable collection | ₹64.8942 cr at 31 March 2026; about 282 closing revenue-days | Customer-confirmed subsequent cash receipts and due-date ageing sufficient to meet obligations | Collections fail the actual debt/property payment calendar, rather than an arbitrary DSO target | Confirmed unrecoverable balances or unavoidable unpaid obligations break a self-funded growth thesis | Next interim accounts, debtor confirmations and bank trail |\\n| Cash and debt maturity | March cash ₹0.0094 cr; debt ₹18.9613 cr; current headroom ND | Verified current cash plus committed facilities covers each dated obligation | Any event requires uncommitted warrant/equity/bank finance to remain solvent | An evidenced unavoidable shortfall would move G3 from Hold to funding failure | Bank cash, sanction/maturity documents and next financial filing |\\n| Property deployment | 16 October agreement ₹10 cr plus charges; payment/status ND | Verified instalments, funding, title, readiness and incremental operating plan | Cash paid or contractually due without matched available funding; capex does not produce the stated operating milestone | Documented payment default or uneconomic deployment that destroys recoverable equity | Payment/registration evidence and each subsequent operating update |\\n| Warrants and ownership claims | 20 October 20,992,160 shares; 13,684,000 warrants remain | Match each allotment to actual 75% receipt; clarify subsidiary ownership/transition | Reliance on optional proceeds before exercise, or unexplained share/NCI differences | Material undisclosed dilution/claims or a funding gap on non-exercise requires reassessment | Each allotment/lapse filing; exercise expiry 18 months after individual January 2026 issue |\\n| Controls and minority treatment | Audit-trail/IFC/CARO/RPT contradictions; proposed MD salary ₹60 lakh | Written reconciliation, actual remuneration/RPT ledger and corrected control disclosure | Repetition of material contradictions or undisclosed related transfers | Substantiated abuse/default, not an unverified suspicion, triggers governance/funding action | Auditor/secretarial responses, next annual/interim report |\\n| Execution and tradability | Capacity last evidenced FY24; subsidiary/property outcomes not verified; ₹52 last trade 14 October | Dated physical/customer milestones plus complete 20/60-session BSE liquidity and current restrictions | Announced milestones lack evidence; requested reference exit cannot be supported at 5% participation | A genuine suspension/effective inability to exit prevents new-purchase eligibility regardless of model value | Each operating update and before considering an actual trade; no monitoring automation created |\\n\\nNo arbitrary price alone clears these conditions. Downward price moves may improve hypothetical valuation while worsening liquidity or signalling missing information. Upward moves do not validate the financials.\\n\\n\"","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\n**Hold — unresolved financial reliability, funding and tradability. No favorable investment classification and no implementation conclusion.** BQS, GQS and IAS remain NS; mathematical bounds are not rankings. The company is not established as a compounder or a funded turnaround, and severe wrongdoing has not been proven.\\n\\nThe next useful action is evidence repair, not an assumed purchase: obtain a coherent financial/cash/issuance reconciliation, validate current solvency and property funding, clarify subsidiary economics, then verify tradability. Only after those are resolved can a supported return model and price-dependent IAS be considered. This is research, not personalized financial advice or an instruction to transact.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\nPrimary issuer PDFs were downloaded and read; selected source facts, page locations and hashes are retained. Originals and OCR are deliberately excluded from the distributable package. Mirrors host original signed filings, not secondary rewritten financial tables. Secondary prices and the subsidiary-ownership summary are clearly labelled; they do not cure missing primary evidence.\\n\\n- [ar2022](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Annual-Report_2021-22.pdf): 37 pages;  image-only originals reviewed separately through audit 2022\\n- [ar2023](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Annual-Report_2022-23.pdf): 50 pages;  image-only originals reviewed separately through audit 2023\\n- [ar2024](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Annual-Report_2023-24.pdf): 43 pages;  audit and financial statements, original vintage\\n- [ar2025](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Annual-Report_2024-25.pdf): printed 36–44 audit/CARO/IFC; 45–47 financials; 48–63 notes\\n- [prospectus](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Prospectus.pdf): printed 29–34 risk; 70–75 objects; 99–115 business; F1–F18 financial/restatement; 195 debt\\n- [ar2026](https://cdn.financialreports.eu/financialreports/media/filings/59667/2026/RNS/59667_rns_2026-09-08_910e84ad-2545-4571-aa9a-0ebd89e40bbb.pdf): printed 55–63 audit/CARO/IFC; 64–66 statements; 80–98 notes; 17–24 board; 46–50 secretarial/remuneration\\n- [security](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz): 19 Oct 2026 official NSE interoperability; SM/ST, lot 3000, stale issued 18438160, not BSE price/trading history\\n- [q1](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/14082026/67f6f69b-0c14-4b2d-94c1-79c1920f9105.pdf): PDF 2–3 standalone review; 4 standalone results visually read; 5–7 consolidated review; 8 results\\n- [allot_oct20](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/20102026/a426f9a8-c13f-40c2-8ac3-cc3cffebf927.pdf): PDF 1–4: 504,000 shares,9828000 rupees received, total 20992160, 18-month exercise term\\n- [allot_aug13](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/13082026/abe44e17-4e7d-4a79-b6e8-fd545b263b05.pdf): PDF 1–4: 680,000 shares, total 20488160\\n- [allot_jun18](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/18062026/45b6067d-9c6d-4bda-9ec9-08adf5b9eda5.pdf): PDF 1–4: 272,000 shares, total 19808160\\n- [fy26_revised](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/24072026/ff4ae039-876d-4882-9a70-dd46441937db.pdf): 24 July resubmission in SME half-year format, unmodified opinion, not a reliable cash-flow reconciliation\\n- [secretarial](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/15052026/19b74446-8647-46e5-ab08-5ae197832a7c.pdf): entire report;  cover wrongly says 532911 but inner report names Retaggio;  no general compliance clearance inferred\\n- [postal](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/04052026/7c9ccec5-5071-4e05-8778-e6d9dd51b6ee.pdf): 15 April notice circulated 4 May; remuneration explanatory statement; excipients boilerplate not jewellery evidence\\n- [ar2026_bs](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/08092026/058454db-989e-4b1f-a2c8-170000142a1d.pdf): same issuer annual report, original BSE filing mirrored without rewriting\\n- [property_bs](https://bsmedia.business-standard.com/_media/bs/data/announcements/bse/16102026/f2a156c3-c697-4ea4-896d-697984693471.pdf): PDF 1–4: agreement dated 16 Oct 2026,10 crore plus actual charges,3261.65 sqft\\n- [audit2022](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/Auditors-Report_Financial-Statement_21-22.pdf): original full independent audit and annexures\\n- [audit2023](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/Audit-Report-and-Financial-Statement_22-23.pdf): original full independent audit and annexures\\n- [audit2024](https://retaggioindustries.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Financials-statements-for-FY-23-24.pdf): original full independent audit and annexures\\n- [market_iifl](https://www.indiainfoline.com/company/retaggio-industries-ltd-historical-data): last trade 14 Oct 2026 at 52;  no inferred 19 Oct closing trade\\n- [market_equitypandit](https://www.equitypandit.com/share-price/92685): last trade 14 Oct 2026 at 52;  no inferred 19 Oct closing trade\\n- [market_screener](https://www.screener.in/company/544391/): last trade 14 Oct 2026 at 52;  no inferred 19 Oct closing trade\\n- [subsidiary_secondary](https://au.marketscreener.com/news/retaggio-industries-limited-approves-incorporation-of-under-name-of-subsidiary-lodha-heritage-privat-ce7f59dadf8df725): 15 April 2026 announcement summary; 76% subscription; not assumed 100% owned\\n- [peer_sky25](https://skygold.co.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annual-Report_Sky-Gold-and-Diamonds-Limited-2024-25.pdf): PDF 3, 5, 7, 30; printed 58–59 financial highlights and printed 180 consolidated P&L\\n- [peer_rbz25](https://rbzjewellers.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Annaul-Report-2024-2025.pdf): PDF 9–10 operating model; PDF 32 financial results; revised report 10 September 2025\\n- [peer_moksh25](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/AR_28914_MOKSH_2024_2025_A_2812467_08092025171608.pdf): PDF 56 main P&L; PDF 66 Note 22; PDF 16 board headline retained as a discrepancy\\n\\n`source_access_log.json` records current official-access outcomes, not a claim that unavailable bars are zero. `historical_inputs.json` preserves original/restated/comparative vintages and original-statement factual extracts. `audit_evidence.json` preserves contrary evidence and unresolved issues. `factor_evidence.json` supplies at least two facts, a contrary fact, source identifiers, confidence and rationale for every factor. `score_audit.json` gives exact contributions, caps and bounds. `model_inputs.json` contains every minimal forward assumption; no private helper, raw PDF, OCR, signed download URL or service identifier is required to rebuild.\\n\\nOpen questions include the complete cash-premium-issuance bridge, corrected audit/control text, current secured-loan terms and property payments, receivable confirmations and loss allowances, actual normalized tax, customer/product physical volumes, related-party cash and remuneration reconciliation, subsidiary ownership/transition, minority claims and current exchange execution conditions. Source publication and model assumptions are not interchangeable evidence.\\n\\n### Revision and final framework check\\n\\nOn 23 October 2026, the initial draft was revised to add the missing three named primary-sourced peers and explicit dated execution, current-share valuation, growth/addition, counterargument and monitoring presentations. Model assumptions, all factor scores, all four gate statuses and the thesis are unchanged. `framework_checklist.json` maps all 15 sections and identifies unavailable metrics rather than silently omitting them. Peer financials are selected FY25 facts, not claims of current peer quality.\\n\\nBoth compact and full backups are standalone. Guarded reconstruction removes only the declared generated outputs, including validation and output-hash attestations, then rebuilds from authored inputs and standard-library code. A baseline manifest retained outside the rebuild directory is compared afterward. Source hashes and successful reconstruction establish provenance and computational consistency, not truth of unresolved company claims.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"end\"","\"original_heading\"","45","69","1460","\"## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\"","1509","4404","\"## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\"","4442","7920","\"## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\"","7944","10101","\"## 4. Business Overview\\n\"","10132","15058","\"## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\"","15087","17733","\"## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\"","17776","19753","\"## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\"","19794","26292","\"## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\"","26318","32730","\"## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\"","32769","44242","\"## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\"","44281","46304","\"## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\"","46332","48012","\"## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\"","48035","51519","\"## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\"","51568","52304","\"## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\"","52349","59912","\"## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\"","\"filename\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"historical_inputs.json\"","\"e9cab3ad79e150ee33f1d2d0c1db16181f3039e171f7c4883e40fcad2fa33d5c\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"29005d8668dcdfebb9730d8458ca7a602251eac851c978fab58f4098b5bb314b\"","\"Authored analytical table or source register, not a bundled original filing. Source-access qualifications remain in the research.\"","\"content\"","\"model_inputs.json\"","\"fcbac99759c23c04175a4c221a760985dfb49fa8b9529cdab8d665690fa11174\"","\"511ca448f7c25d99d394b820a006545b403f5a702c540a8625de57b2354b34c4\"","\"factor_evidence.json\"","\"77e93541c39e2eb8782223074a613b6e2c73dcf3a3700f81642ffea1f6e4d9df\"","\"c64c5a4048483d5bd2c3224525c77564b18639d2df72c24244ab8278119330ea\"","\"audit_evidence.json\"","\"7ffdb612c624dc0fb7d341f739c30dcfb58433cfcbaee900a838db3f1083ef6f\"","\"8014c1c837b997580f9de597f706a3c6e05dd82fa9e580fef0c02c7d8214a009\"","\"market_extract.json\"","\"9d2ca4313e50f0ae577befa5d26000b091a9a188b176a6ae462117da7cbf14ea\"","\"f768798bd6068cf0e518765250a21bcc3fa0632dd3392833ee083750b4f5db5f\"","\"security_extract.json\"","\"dd5411d04fa16d180694ea344615f121faf29198aa03761e9651d36f5abc9e9f\"","\"3af56e13c69712ea619176e66c17d70537df08290956d2b1875735ad4b15dc5f\"","\"source_register.json\"","\"2aaf4eae1b31fc8ad64198128c0c091789ae1773d36dadd92b65e63ac14e6687\"","\"a9b0cd56bae141049bb732cbd557a785204d1905460b8daf6b2aadec58